.TE
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION LIFECYCLE
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Invocation and input selection\f[R] \- The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary
resolves \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R] and command\-line flags, sets the initial
\f[CR]mood\f[R] from \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] or the invocation basename
(\f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]dash\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]rbash\f[R]), and
selects the input source in strict priority order (\f[CR]\-s\f[R],
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], script file operand, \f[CR]\-i\f[R] or terminal stdin,
or non\-interactive stdin).
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Configuration and startup loading\f[R] \- Unless \f[CR]\-Q\f[R],
rescue mode, \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[R], a secure (setuid or setgid)
execution, or a \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] or utility invocation skips them, the
shell loads \f[CR]/etc/kosh.conf\f[R] (or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[R]),
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] (or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R]), and \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R], then
sources the startup files for the active \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] or
session mood.
\f[CR]\-\-no\-config\f[R] and privileged mode (\f[CR]\-p\f[R]) skip only
parts of this sequence, as listed under Startup bypass.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Lexing, parsing, and static analysis\f[R] \- The parser builds an
abstract syntax tree (AST) and reports all syntax or dialect errors in a
single pass.
In the default \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, the static analyzer inspects the
AST before execution and rejects scripts with \f[CR]strict\f[R] or
\f[CR]lenient\f[R] errors (exit status \f[CR]1\f[R]).
In the compatibility moods, analysis runs only when \f[CR]\-W\f[R] or
\f[CR]set \-W\f[R] enables it, and its findings are warnings that do not
stop the script.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Word expansion\f[R] \- For each simple command, words expand in
fixed order: brace expansion, tilde expansion,
parameter/arithmetic/command/process expansion (left to right), field
splitting (\f[CR]IFS\f[R]), pathname globbing, and quote removal.
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]Command resolution and dispatch\f[R] \- Commands containing a slash
execute directly.
Words without a slash resolve in strict order: shell functions, enabled
builtins, hashed \f[CR]PATH\f[R] entries, \f[CR]PATH\f[R] search, and
bundled \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] utilities when the mood,
\f[CR]\-\-enable\-koshkit\f[R], or \f[CR]enable\f[R] makes them
reachable.
Interactive shells with \f[CR]autocd\f[R] enabled fall back to changing
directories if the word matches a directory path.
