.TE
.SS Options and settings reference
Command\-line flags override both settings files and
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
Runtime settings are read and mutated through \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]shopt\f[R], and \f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
See the kosh(5) configuration manual for the exhaustive list of
command\-line flags, option IDs, namespaces, aliases, and mutability
restrictions.
.SS Static analysis and runtime diagnostics
All diagnostics write to standard error in an identical format across
every mood (with colored labels and codes on color\-capable terminals).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Diagnostic layout\f[R] \- Each entry prints
\f[CR]file:line:column: severity: message\f[R], with the check code in
parentheses on an analysis finding (\f[CR](SC2086)\f[R]), followed by
the source line, a caret (\f[CR]\[ha]\f[R]) under the offending column,
and optional \f[CR]note:\f[R] remediation lines.
Dynamic runtime sources prefix locations as \f[CR]eval:line:col:\f[R],
\f[CR]trap:line:col:\f[R], or \f[CR]\-c:line:col:\f[R], while stdin and
interactive lines omit the source label.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Stack backtraces\f[R] \- Each active function call, sourced file,
\f[CR]eval\f[R], trap action, subshell, and command/function/process
substitution between the top level and the failing command appends a
\f[CR]trace:\f[R] frame.
Consecutive identical frames collapse into a single frame with a repeat
count.
When multiple \f[CR]\-c\f[R] strings or files are processed, the
top\-level command\-line frame prints once after all diagnostics for
that input.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Multi\-error syntax reporting\f[R] \- Linting, the language server,
and script execution report all syntax errors in a single pass.
Dialect violations (such as \f[CR][[\f[R] in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood) are
reported while still parsing the inner contents for additional syntax
errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Pre\-run static analysis tiers\f[R] \- Analysis categorizes checks
into \f[CR]strict\f[R], \f[CR]lenient\f[R], and \f[CR]annoying\f[R]
tiers (configured via \f[CR]\-W\f[R] and listed via
\f[CR]\-\-list\-diagnostics\f[R]).
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, analysis runs automatically before
execution; \f[CR]strict\f[R] and \f[CR]lenient\f[R] findings abort the
script with exit status \f[CR]1\f[R] before any command executes.
.IP \[bu] 2
In compatibility moods, analysis runs only when enabled by
\f[CR]\-W\f[R] or \f[CR]set \-W\f[R], and all findings emit as
non\-fatal warnings.
When the warning level is nonzero, script operands lacking a shebang are
flagged.
.IP \[bu] 2
Interactive shells analyze each submitted line prior to execution.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Core static checks\f[R] \- Analysis detects variables expanded but
never assigned, functions referencing positional arguments that are
invoked without arguments, functions invoked prior to their definition,
and non\-\f[CR]local\f[R] variable assignments inside functions (in the
\f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, which lacks \f[CR]local\f[R], only variables first
assigned inside a function and referenced outside it are flagged).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Scope and traversal\f[R] \- The analyzer follows static
\f[CR]source\f[R] operands into readable files, evaluates
command/backtick/process substitutions as subshells, and evaluates
\f[CR]${ ...; }\f[R] function substitutions in the current shell
environment.
When multiple findings occur, a summary line
(\f[CR]Encountered M warning and N errors.\f[R]) appends to the output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Inline suppression directives\f[R] \- Comments matching
\f[CR]# shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted\-command\-substitution\f[R]
suppress static analysis findings.
Numeric codes (\f[CR]SC2086\f[R]) suppress all variants under that ID,
slug names suppress only that exact variant, \f[CR]all\f[R] suppresses
all checks, and ranges (\f[CR]SC2000\-SC2100\f[R]) suppress numeric
spans.
A directive placed before the first command applies to the entire file;
placed later, it applies to the immediately following and\-or list.
Directives never suppress syntax or runtime errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Path and internal fault reporting\f[R] \- Missing commands or file
paths report the first non\-existent path component when identifiable.
Internal interpreter faults emit an \f[CR]error:\f[R] line and
\f[CR]note:\f[R] lines with process and build metadata before aborting.
.SS Offline linting and host extraction (\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R])
\f[CR]kosh \-\-lint\f[R] performs static analysis without executing
code, enabling all three diagnostic tiers (\f[CR]strict\f[R],
\f[CR]lenient\f[R], \f[CR]annoying\f[R]) at the severity rules of the
active mood regardless of \f[CR]\-W\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-no\-diagnostics\f[R], or
\f[CR]\-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Input order\f[R] \- Analyzes \f[CR]\-c\f[R] command strings in
order, then file operands, or standard input if neither is provided.
Startup and settings files are skipped.
Diagnostics write to stderr and stdout remains empty.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Polyglot host file extraction\f[R] \- Extracts and analyzes
embedded \f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R], and \f[CR]kosh\f[R] scripts
from CI workflows, Ansible playbooks, Dockerfiles, Docker Compose files,
Markdown documents, Makefiles, justfiles, and Kubernetes manifests.
Files are identified by extension or shebang; unrecognized file formats
fail with status \f[CR]1\f[R] and print the supported format list.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Host interpreter declarations (\f[CR]shell:\f[R] step keys,
\f[CR]SHELL\f[R] instructions, shebangs, or executable flags) select the
analysis mood; fragments targeting non\-shell interpreters are skipped.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]exec\f[R] arrays extract only the script operand of a shell
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] invocation.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]justfile\f[R] recipe lines (joined across \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R]
continuations) are analyzed as individual commands, while shebang or
\f[CR][script]\f[R] recipes are analyzed as single scripts.
.IP \[bu] 2
Jinja shell templates (\f[CR]*.sh.j2\f[R]) and host template
interpolations/editor variables are masked as plain words.
Unresolved command checks are suppressed in a fragment that contains a
host interpolation.
Host\-injected variables (CI variables, exported recipe arguments,
Dockerfile \f[CR]ARG\f[R]/\f[CR]ENV\f[R]) are pre\-marked as assigned.
All diagnostic line and column numbers map back to the outer host file.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Automated remediation (\f[CB]\-\-apply\f[B])\f[R] \- Writes safe
structured AST fixes to file operands, prints a fix summary, and outputs
only remaining unresolvable diagnostics.
If a transformation would produce invalid syntax, the file is left
unmodified.
Exits with status \f[CR]0\f[R] when only warnings remain, and status
\f[CR]1\f[R] on remaining errors or I/O failures.
.SS Source code formatting (\f[CR]\-\-format\f[R])
\f[CR]kosh \-\-format\f[R] normalizes shell source code without
executing it.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Formatting invariants\f[R] \- Places structural keywords and
compound braces on dedicated lines, indents nested blocks by
\f[CR]2\f[R] spaces, rewrites unshadowed \f[CR]test\f[R] invocations
into \f[CR][ ... ]\f[R] bracket commands, wraps lines at \f[CR]80\f[R]
columns at safe token boundaries, and normalizes line endings to
\f[CR]LF\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Input and host behavior\f[R] \- With no file operand or
\f[CR]\-\f[R], reads stdin and writes to stdout (with syntax coloring
when stdout is a terminal).
In polyglot host files, formats only mutable shell regions (such as
Markdown fenced code blocks).
Syntax errors emit diagnostics and exit with status \f[CR]1\f[R].
When combined with \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], safe lint fixes apply before
formatting.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Atomic in\-place updates (\f[CB]\-\-apply\f[B])\f[R] \- Writes
formatted output to a temporary file alongside each target and
atomically renames it over the original, preserving file permissions and
symbolic links.
Rejects non\-regular files and files modified externally during
processing.
.SS Language Server Protocol daemon (\f[CR]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[R])
\f[CR]kosh \-\-as\-language\-server\f[R] serves the LSP specification
over standard input and output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Capabilities\f[R] \- Publishes live diagnostics on document change,
tab completion (triggered by space or \f[CR]\-\f[R]), quick fixes, a
\f[CR]fix\-all\f[R] code action for non\-conflicting safe fixes,
document formatting, go\-to\-definition, hover documentation, semantic
token highlighting, document symbols/outline, and rename within the open
document.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Polyglot and cross\-file resolution\f[R] \- Operates inside
embedded shell regions of all host formats supported by
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R] and follows relative \f[CR]source\f[R] paths from
the document directory.
Rename updates variables, functions, and aliases across the open
document while preserving \f[CR]$name\f[R] vs \f[CR]${name}\f[R]
bracing; commands can be renamed only when defined as a function or
alias within the open document.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Document mood resolution\f[R] \- Resolves mood in priority order:
document shebang, LSP language ID (\f[CR]kosh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R],
\f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], where \f[CR]rbash\f[R] and
\f[CR]shellscript\f[R] map to \f[CR]bash\f[R]), file extension
(\f[CR].kosh\f[R], \f[CR].bash\f[R], \f[CR].sh\f[R], \f[CR].dash\f[R]),
or fallback \f[CR]kosh\f[R].
Skips startup/settings files and exits with status \f[CR]0\f[R] on clean
LSP shutdown or \f[CR]1\f[R] otherwise.
.SS Startup and configuration file loading
Every shell instance loads declarative settings before sourcing startup
scripts:
.IP "1." 3
System settings file: \f[CR]/etc/kosh.conf\f[R] (POSIX) or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[R] (Windows).
.IP "2." 3
User settings file: \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] (skipped when the POSIX
effective user is \f[CR]root\f[R]).
.IP "3." 3
Environment payload: \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] (unset from the environment
immediately after ingestion).
.PP
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, login and interactive shells source
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R] (or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R]); no
\f[CR]/etc/koshrc\f[R] or \f[CR]\[ti]/.koshrc\f[R] exists, and the POSIX
\f[CR]root\f[R] user skips user \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R].
In interactive non\-login \f[CR]kosh\f[R] shells, \f[CR]\-\-norc\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rcfile\f[R], and \f[CR]\-p\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-privileged\f[R])
govern \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] identically to \f[CR]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R].
Any Bash startup file sourced from \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] automatically
executes under the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood and returns to \f[CR]kosh\f[R]
upon completion (see \f[CR]source\f[R]).
Passing \f[CR]\-L\f[R] or setting \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] replaces the
list of moods whose startup files run (for example, \f[CR]\-L bash\f[R]
makes a \f[CR]kosh\f[R] session source only the Bash startup files in
the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood and then return to \f[CR]kosh\f[R], while
\f[CR]\-L bash,kosh\f[R] also sources \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] afterwards).
A mood set in a settings file selects the startup files only when no
\f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R],
invocation basename, or script shebang selects one (see kosh(5) for the
precedence), and command\-line options keep their values over settings
files.
Compatibility moods use the files described under Bash and POSIX startup
files.
.SS Startup bypass and post\-startup enforcement (\f[CR]skipping\-startup\-files\f[R])
.PP
.TS
