.TE
.SS Interactive line editing
The interactive line editor defaults to \f[CR]emacs\f[R] mode
(\f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R] and \f[CR]set \-o emacs\f[R] switch modes).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Viewport and rendering\f[R] \- Multi\-line buffers taller than the
terminal viewport scroll around the cursor row, reserving \f[CR]2\f[R]
bottom rows for hints and hiding ghost text and the right prompt.
Control bytes render in caret notation (\f[CR]\[ha][\f[R] for
\f[CR]ESC\f[R]), filenames containing control bytes complete in
\f[CR]$\[aq]...\[aq]\f[R] syntax, and bracketed paste inserts text
literally without executing newlines.
When the cursor rests on or immediately after an unquoted, unescaped
bracket \f[CR]( ) [ ] { }\f[R] outside comments and here\-documents, its
matching partner highlights.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Kill ring, undo, and external editing\f[R] \- Kill operations push
erased text onto a 16\-entry session kill ring, merging consecutive
kills into a single entry.
\f[CR]Ctrl\-K\f[R] on an empty line joins the next line, or deletes an
empty final line.
Undo reverts a yank or \f[CR]Alt\-.\f[R] insertion and all its cycled
replacements in one step.
\f[CR]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E\f[R] (or \f[CR]:e\f[R] in Vi mode) opens the
current buffer in \f[CR]$VISUAL\f[R], \f[CR]$EDITOR\f[R], or
\f[CR]vi\f[R]; exiting the external editor with status \f[CR]0\f[R]
replaces the buffer without executing it, while nonzero exits preserve
the original buffer and print a message.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Extended key reporting and undo/redo bindings\f[R] \- While
collecting input, \f[CR]Ctrl\-Z\f[R] undoes edits (during command
execution, \f[CR]Ctrl\-Z\f[R] suspends the foreground job).
\f[CR]Ctrl\-Shift\-Z\f[R] redoes edits when reported distinctly via the
kitty keyboard protocol or xterm \f[CR]modifyOtherKeys\f[R] (forwarded
by \f[CR]tmux\f[R] when \f[CR]extended\-keys\f[R] is \f[CR]on\f[R]).
Disable protocol requests via
\f[CR]koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off\f[R].
On the Windows console, both \f[CR]Ctrl\-Z\f[R] and
\f[CR]Ctrl\-Shift\-Z\f[R] undo, and \f[CR]Ctrl\-\[ha]\f[R] redoes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Prefix history recall and auto\-pairing\f[R] \- With
\f[CR]editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix=on\f[R]
(default), pressing \f[CR]Up\f[R] on a non\-empty line recalls only
history entries matching the prefix preceding the first \f[CR]Up\f[R]
press, while an empty line steps through all history.
Setting \f[CR]editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes=on\f[R]
automatically inserts matching closers for \f[CR](\f[R], \f[CR][\f[R],
\f[CR]{\f[R], \f[CR]\[aq]\f[R], and \f[CR]\[dq]\f[R], steps over typed
closers, and deletes empty pairs on \f[CR]Backspace\f[R].
.SS Live synopsis hints and pre\-run diagnostics
When the cursor advances past the command word, up to \f[CR]16\f[R] hint
rows appear below the input line (hidden while completion menus or
searches are active).
A keystroke never runs the command itself, and a Tab that needs an
uncached documentation load shows \f[CR]loading...\f[R] until the load
finishes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Command synopsis
(\f[CB]editor.hints.show_command_synopsis=on\f[B])\f[R] \- Displays
command, subcommand, and flag documentation under \f[CR]synopsis\f[R],
marking the active argument with \f[CR]>\f[R].
Sources data from builtin/\f[CR]koshkit\f[R] help, alias definitions,
function source locations, section 1 man pages (\f[CR]man\f[R] /
\f[CR]manpath\f[R]), subcommand man pages (\f[CR]git\-commit(1)\f[R]),
or \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] output for allowlisted binaries.
External \f[CR]man\f[R] and allowlisted binaries execute only from
absolute directories owned by the user or \f[CR]root\f[R] and not
writable by group or others (never from empty or relative
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] entries).
Pending multi\-key prefixes (\f[CR]Ctrl\-X\f[R] or Vi operators) display
valid completion keys.
Disabling the option also stops its documentation loads.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Live diagnostics
(\f[CB]editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics=on\f[B])\f[R] \- Displays the
first static analysis finding (\f[CR]error\f[R], \f[CR]warning\f[R],
\f[CR]note\f[R]) or parse error (\f[CR]syntax error\f[R]) after a brief
pause without opening files, following \f[CR]source\f[R] targets, or
looking up commands.
Because pressing \f[CR]Enter\f[R] submits the entire buffer without a
\f[CR]PS2\f[R] continuation prompt, incomplete constructs report
immediately as syntax errors.
Disabling both hint options also hides pending\-key hints.
.SS Tab completion and candidate selectors
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Ghost text\f[R] \- Suggests inline prefix matches from completion
and history; \f[CR]Right\f[R] or \f[CR]End\f[R] at the end of the buffer
accepts the full suggestion, and \f[CR]Ctrl\-Right\f[R] or
\f[CR]Alt\-F\f[R] accepts the next word.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Context\-sensitive candidate generation\f[R] \- \f[CR]Tab\f[R]
computes exact\-prefix, smart\-case, and subsequence matches:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Empty command word: shell keywords, builtins, functions, aliases,
bundled utilities, and \f[CR]PATH\f[R] executables.
.IP \[bu] 2
Arguments: file paths (directories append \f[CR]/\f[R]), \f[CR]$var\f[R]
/ \f[CR]${var}\f[R] variables, \f[CR]\[ti]user\f[R] accounts, and
\f[CR]\-flag\f[R] options.
.IP \[bu] 2
Specialized commands: programmable \f[CR]complete\f[R] specs take top
priority; \f[CR]cd\f[R] and \f[CR]pushd\f[R] complete directories across
\f[CR]CDPATH\f[R] (unless starting with \f[CR]/\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R], or
\f[CR]\[ti]\f[R]); \f[CR]make\f[R] extracts targets statically from
\f[CR]Makefile\f[R]; \f[CR]cmake \-\-build <dir> \-\-target\f[R]
extracts targets from \f[CR]<dir>/CMakeFiles/TargetDirectories.txt\f[R]
without executing \f[CR]make\f[R], \f[CR]cmake\f[R], or
\f[CR]$(shell ...)\f[R].
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Insertion and selector modes (\f[CB]\-\-tab\-selector\f[B] /
\f[CB]completion.menu_style\f[B])\f[R] \- Pressing \f[CR]Tab\f[R]
inserts the longest common prefix.
A single match replaces the token and appends a space according to
\f[CR]completion.add_space_after_completed_word\f[R] (\f[CR]off\f[R]
default, \f[CR]on\f[R], or \f[CR]on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash\f[R]).
Multiple candidates open the configured selector:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Selector mode
T}@T{
Completion and \f[CR]Ctrl\-R\f[R] history search behavior
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]interactive\f[R] (default)
T}@T{
Renders an in\-editor menu under \f[CR]selecting completions\f[R] or
\f[CR]incremental history search\f[R].
Typing filters candidates (commands, paths, and \f[CR]koshconf\f[R]
options retain the highest non\-empty tier among exact\-prefix,
smart\-case\-prefix, and subsequence matches; other lists use
case\-insensitive prefix matching; history matches prefix, substring, or
ordered subsequence bytes).
\f[CR]Backspace\f[R] widens results; typing space, \f[CR]/\f[R], or
deleting past token start refreshes candidates; accepting a directory
opens its contents.
T}
T{
\f[CR]external\f[R]
T}@T{
Pipes multiple candidates into \f[CR]$KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND\f[R]
(\f[CR]fzf\f[R] default) and replaces the token with stdout.
A single candidate or a session without a controlling terminal uses the
\f[CR]plain\f[R] listing.
An empty or missing selector command, or an exit status other than
\f[CR]0\f[R], \f[CR]1\f[R], or \f[CR]130\f[R], uses the \f[CR]plain\f[R]
listing after a message.
Status \f[CR]1\f[R] or \f[CR]130\f[R] dismisses the selector and leaves
the line unchanged.
T}
T{
\f[CR]plain\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints candidates in a multi\-column list (always used on terminals
lacking decoration support) and keeps \f[CR]Ctrl\-R\f[R] history search
inline.
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]History search (\f[CB]Ctrl\-R\f[B])\f[R] \- Searches a snapshot of
the shared history file newest\-first through the selected selector with
the current line as the initial query, without mutating the in\-memory
private history list.
An accepted entry replaces the line.
.SS Terminal shell integration marks
Interactive sessions attached to a terminal emit \f[CR]OSC 133\f[R]
(\f[CR]A\f[R], \f[CR]B\f[R], \f[CR]C\f[R], \f[CR]D\f[R])
prompt/command/status marks, \f[CR]OSC 7\f[R] working directory URIs,
and \f[CR]OSC 633\f[R] marks when \f[CR]TERM_PROGRAM=vscode\f[R].
Emission is disabled when \f[CR]TERM=dumb\f[R] or
\f[CR]KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION=0\f[R].
.SS Editor keybinding reference
.SS Emacs mode and Vi insert mode keys
Vi insert mode supports all Emacs bindings below except
\f[CR]Ctrl\-V\f[R], \f[CR]Alt\-X\f[R], and \f[CR]Ctrl\-X\f[R] chords;
arrow keys in Vi insert mode recall history, and \f[CR]Escape\f[R]
transitions to Vi command mode (moving 1 character left).
Unlisted \f[CR]Shift\f[R] or \f[CR]Alt\f[R] combinations with
\f[CR]Up\f[R], \f[CR]Down\f[R], \f[CR]Left\f[R], \f[CR]Right\f[R],
\f[CR]Home\f[R], or \f[CR]End\f[R] behave as the unmodified key, and
\f[CR]Ctrl\-Backspace\f[R] deletes 1 character left.
.PP
.TS
