.TE
.SS Native Koshka builtins
The six native Koshka builtins (\f[CR]assimilate\f[R], \f[CR]bench\f[R],
\f[CR]koshconf\f[R], \f[CR]koshkit\f[R], \f[CR]slurp\f[R], \f[CR]z\f[R])
are available in every mood.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Builtin and synopsis
T}@T{
Operational specification and exit codes
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]assimilate [options] [TARGET]\f[R]
T}@T{
Deploys the running \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary locally (when
\f[CR]TARGET\f[R] is omitted) or to a remote host over \f[CR]scp\f[R] as
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] (\f[CR]kosh.exe\f[R] for a local Windows install) in the
first writable \f[CR]PATH\f[R] directory, preferring
\f[CR]/usr/local/bin\f[R], then \f[CR]$HOME/.local/bin\f[R], when on
\f[CR]PATH\f[R], and excluding \f[CR]sbin\f[R] paths unless
\f[CR]\-\-install\-directory=DIR\f[R] is passed.
Locally, links each bundled utility and utility alias whose name is free
or already links to \f[CR]kosh\f[R] (relative symlink on POSIX,
\f[CR].exe\f[R] hard link on Windows) and each
\f[CR]\-\-link\-mood=MOOD\f[R] name (\f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]dash\f[R],
\f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]kosh\f[R]; repeatable, comma\-separated values).
Utility names held by another entry in the directory or provided by a
program elsewhere on \f[CR]PATH\f[R] stay unlinked, with one warning per
group; a conflicting \f[CR]\-\-link\-mood\f[R] name rejects the
directory (POSIX) or returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (Windows).
A later Windows run with a changed binary links again.
Options: \f[CR]\-\-ssh\-command=CMD\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-scp\-command=CMD\f[R] (replace \f[CR]ssh\f[R] and
\f[CR]scp\f[R]; require \f[CR]TARGET\f[R], else status \f[CR]2\f[R]),
\f[CR]\-x\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-trace\f[R] prints progress), and
\f[CR]\-y\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] skips the confirmation, described
under Bundled Koshkit utilities, that names the target and the full path
of the new binary; a declined question warns and returns \f[CR]1\f[R]).
Validates the copied binary before atomic replacement and restores the
previous file or link on failure.
T}
T{
\f[CR]bench [options] command ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Benchmarks each \f[CR]command\f[R] via \f[CR]kosh \-c\f[R] and reports
mean, standard deviation, and range for wall time, peak RSS, and
platform performance counters, and notes outlier samples after the
report.
Stops sampling on nonzero command exit unless
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-exit\-code\f[R] is set, and returns that exit status
(\f[CR]130\f[R] on interrupt).
A missing command, an invalid or out\-of\-range count, or \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
with \f[CR]\-n\f[R] returns \f[CR]1\f[R]; an unknown flag returns
\f[CR]2\f[R].
Options: \f[CR]\-\-show\-output\f[R], \f[CR]\-N\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-no\-shell\f[R] direct exec), \f[CR]\-n\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-runs\f[R] \f[CR]1\f[R]..\f[CR]100000\f[R]), \f[CR]\-t\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-duration\f[R] ms, default \f[CR]3000\f[R]), \f[CR]\-w\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-warmup\f[R] \f[CR]1\f[R]..\f[CR]100000\f[R]; without argument
runs 1\-3 warmups until two agree within 10%; default 1 warmup),
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-profile\f[R], alias for
\f[CR]\-\-runs 1 \-\-extended\f[R]), and \f[CR]\-E\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-extended\f[R] CPU/wait time, context switches, faults, I/O,
waiting time, and block and read/write call counts, plus Linux scheduler
and hardware counters and traced syscalls/threads/execs/task counts from
one extra untimed run, macOS processor counters, run\-queue wait,
wakeups, energy, and storage figures, and Windows Job Object
process\-tree counters).
Traced rows are marked \f[CR](traced)\f[R], a row marked \f[CR]*\f[R]
counts only the measured process, and a row the platform cannot report
is omitted.
T}
T{
\f[CR]koshconf <subcommand> ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Manages configuration settings:\- \f[CR]create PRESET [\-\-force]\f[R]:
Writes the default file of the \f[CR]kosh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R], or
\f[CR]sh\f[R] preset to user \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R] with every setting
except the mood commented out (and default \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] if
missing); refuses to replace an existing file without
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R].\- \f[CR]set OPTION VALUE [\-\-persist]\f[R]:
Mutates session option, and with \f[CR]\-\-persist\f[R] also rewrites
its line in user \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R] (saving the previous file as
\f[CR]kosh.conf.bak\f[R]).\- \f[CR]get OPTION\f[R] /
\f[CR]list [\-a|\-\-all]\f[R]: Prints single value or alphabetical
\f[CR]name=value\f[R] list (\f[CR]\-a\f[R] adds a line for each alias
and for the \f[CR]set\f[R] options \f[CR]hashall\f[R] and
\f[CR]nolog\f[R], which only \f[CR]set\f[R] accepts).\-
\f[CR]load ENCODED\f[R] / \f[CR]save [\-\-persist]\f[R]: Applies or
outputs the \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] Base64 payload, so a shell started with
\f[CR]KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)\f[R] adopts the settings
(\f[CR]save \-\-persist\f[R] writes each setting that differs from the
system file, or from the mood default where the system file does not set
it, to \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R]).Booleans are \f[CR]on\f[R] or
\f[CR]off\f[R], also accepted as \f[CR]true\f[R], \f[CR]false\f[R],
\f[CR]1\f[R], or \f[CR]0\f[R].
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] on success, \f[CR]1\f[R] on an unknown option or
value or an unreadable or unwritable file, and \f[CR]2\f[R] on invalid
command syntax.
An interactive shell reports the written file on standard error.
Restricted shells reject \f[CR]create\f[R], \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]load\f[R], and \f[CR]save \-\-persist\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]koshkit [\-\-color when] [utility [arg ...]]\f[R]
T}@T{
Invokes a bundled utility (\f[CR]\-\-color\f[R] takes \f[CR]always\f[R],
\f[CR]never\f[R], or \f[CR]auto\f[R]), or prints the categorized utility
catalog when invoked without arguments (\f[CR]\-\-list\f[R] prints one
utility per line; \f[CR]\-\-list\-flags\f[R] prints utilities and
flags).
T}
T{
\f[CR]slurp [\-e|\-v|\-V] [+LINE] FILE ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Opens each \f[CR]FILE\f[R] in turn in the full\-screen alternate\-buffer
editor (\f[CR]\-e\f[R] Emacs mode, \f[CR]\-v\f[R] Vi insert mode,
\f[CR]\-V\f[R] Vi normal mode on the first line; \f[CR]+LINE\f[R] opens
the next \f[CR]FILE\f[R] at that line).
Highlights shell scripts (recognized by shebang or extension) and writes
files atomically, preserving final newlines, permissions, and symlinks.
A missing \f[CR]FILE\f[R] opens empty and is created on save.
An empty operand, directory, special or unreadable file, file containing
\f[CR]NUL\f[R] or invalid UTF\-8, or file over \f[CR]16 MiB\f[R] is
rejected, and the next \f[CR]FILE\f[R] still opens.
A restricted shell or non\-terminal standard streams fail the run.
The status is \f[CR]1\f[R] on a rejection or failed run, \f[CR]2\f[R] on
usage errors (a missing \f[CR]FILE\f[R], a \f[CR]+LINE\f[R] without a
\f[CR]FILE\f[R], or more than one mode option), and \f[CR]130\f[R] on
\f[CR]Ctrl\-C\f[R], which ends the run.
The bundled \f[CR]vi\f[R] and \f[CR]emacs\f[R] run \f[CR]slurp \-V\f[R]
and \f[CR]slurp \-e\f[R].
See the \f[CR]slurp\f[R] bullets below.
T}
T{
\f[CR]z [query ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Jumps to a directory recorded in \f[CR]$KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY\f[R].
A single operand that is an existing directory is used as it is;
otherwise the operands form one case\-insensitive substring query ranked
by visit count and recency; no operand jumps to \f[CR]$HOME\f[R].
Returns \f[CR]1\f[R] when no match exists.
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp\f[B] screen\f[R] \- The top row repeats the prompt and
the command, the file fills the rows below, and the last two rows show
the file kind, line endings, size, and caret position, then the key set
and the last save result or the keys of the key menu.
A hint, message, or command line that needs more rows takes up to eight
while it shows.
Tabs and trailing blanks are drawn shaded.
A shell script keeps syntax highlighting, and the format commands change
only such a file.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp\f[B] input\f[R] \- \f[CR]Enter\f[R] starts a new line,
\f[CR]Up\f[R] and \f[CR]Down\f[R] do not recall history, and
\f[CR]Tab\f[R] inserts a tab at the start of a line or in a file that is
not a shell script, and completes otherwise.
The mouse moves the caret, selects by dragging, and scrolls with the
wheel; with the Emacs key set, \f[CR]Shift\f[R] with a movement key also
selects.
\f[CR]:N\f[R] and \f[CR]:$\f[R] move to line \f[CR]N\f[R] and the last
line, \f[CR]Ctrl\-S\f[R] and \f[CR]Ctrl\-R\f[R] (or \f[CR]/\f[R] and
\f[CR]?\f[R]) find text as it is typed, and \f[CR]help\f[R] in
\f[CR]M\-x\f[R] and \f[CR]:help\f[R] list the commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp\f[B] saving and leaving\f[R] \- \f[CR]Ctrl\-X s\f[R],
\f[CR]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S\f[R], \f[CR]:w\f[R], and \f[CR]:write\f[R] save
and keep editing; \f[CR]:wq\f[R], \f[CR]:wq!\f[R], \f[CR]:wqa\f[R],
\f[CR]:wqa!\f[R], and \f[CR]:x\f[R] save and close the file when the
save succeeds; \f[CR]:q\f[R], \f[CR]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C\f[R],
\f[CR]M\-x kill\-kosh\f[R], and \f[CR]Ctrl\-D\f[R] on an empty buffer
close it without saving, and the next \f[CR]FILE\f[R] opens.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
While the buffer has unsaved changes, \f[CR]:q\f[R] refuses and
\f[CR]:q!\f[R] discards; \f[CR]Ctrl\-C\f[R], \f[CR]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C\f[R],
\f[CR]M\-x kill\-kosh\f[R], and \f[CR]Ctrl\-D\f[R] need a second press.
.IP \[bu] 2
A save refuses when another file has replaced the loaded one, and a
failed save is reported while the edits stay open.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp\f[B] write method\f[R] \- Saves through a sibling
temporary file renamed over the original.
A file the user does not own, a file with several links, and a file in a
directory that is owned by neither the user nor root or is group\- or
world\-writable without the sticky bit are rewritten in place instead,
keeping owner, group, and links, after a private backup in
\f[CR]$HOME\f[R] or \f[CR]$TMPDIR\f[R].
The backup is removed after a successful write and reported after a
failed one, and without a backup nothing is written.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp\f[B] line endings\f[R] \- A file keeps CRLF endings
only when every line ends in CRLF; a file that mixes endings keeps each
carriage return as text.
.SS Bundled Koshkit utilities
Koshka bundles 108 utilities plus 4 aliases (\f[CR]egrep\f[R] and
\f[CR]fgrep\f[R] for \f[CR]grep\f[R]; \f[CR]vi\f[R] and \f[CR]emacs\f[R]
for \f[CR]slurp \-V\f[R] and \f[CR]slurp \-e\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Resolution and invocation\f[R] \- In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood (or
when \f[CR]\-\-enable\-koshkit\f[R] /
\f[CR]interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands=on\f[R] is set),
bundled utilities resolve directly as commands \f[I]after\f[R]
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] lookup.
Invoking the binary via a symlink/hardlink named after a utility
(case\-sensitive, stripping \f[CR].exe\f[R]) or as
\f[CR]koshkit <utility>\f[R] executes the utility directly, bypassing
shell startup files, settings files, and \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R].
The \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] link that \f[CR]make install\f[R] places beside
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] runs its first operand as a utility
(\f[CR]koshkit ls \-l\f[R]) and otherwise acts as the \f[CR]koshkit\f[R]
builtin.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Option parsing and exit status invariants\f[R] \- Every utility
accepts \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] (and \f[CR]\-\-version\f[R] when invoked
under its own binary name).
Flags may appear before or after operands (\f[CR]\-\-\f[R] ends option
parsing), except for \f[CR]calc\f[R], \f[CR]env\f[R], \f[CR]expr\f[R],
\f[CR]flock\f[R], \f[CR]nice\f[R], \f[CR]nohup\f[R], \f[CR]retry\f[R],
\f[CR]timeout\f[R], \f[CR]watch\f[R], and \f[CR]xargs\f[R], which stop
parsing options at the first positional operand.
Utilities return \f[CR]0\f[R] on success, \f[CR]1\f[R] on
operand/processing failure (continuing to remaining file operands), and
\f[CR]2\f[R] on usage errors (which terminates scripts in the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood).
An unknown utility returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
Diagnostics are prefixed \f[CR]Koshkit \[ga]seq\[ga]:\f[R] under the
builtin and \f[CR]seq:\f[R] under the utility\[aq]s own name.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Confirmation prompts\f[R] \- A confirmation question
(\f[CR]rm \-i\f[R], \f[CR]cp \-i\f[R], \f[CR]mv \-i\f[R],
\f[CR]assimilate\f[R]) goes to standard error after the shell prefix,
followed by \f[CR][y/N]\f[R] or \f[CR][Y/n]\f[R] with the default
capitalized.
The answer is read from standard input up to a newline: an empty line
takes the default, an answer beginning with \f[CR]y\f[R] or \f[CR]Y\f[R]
confirms, and any other answer or end of input declines.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Catalog and color\f[R] \- \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] with no utility groups
the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings.
Styled reports (\f[CR]cal\f[R], \f[CR]cat\f[R], \f[CR]du\f[R],
\f[CR]ls\f[R], \f[CR]watch\f[R], \f[CR]meow\f[R], and the
\f[CR]evil\f[R] and \f[CR]good\f[R] utilities) use color only when
output is a terminal, \f[CR]NO_COLOR\f[R] is empty or unset, and
\f[CR]TERM\f[R] is not \f[CR]dumb\f[R].
\f[CR]koshkit \-\-color when\f[R] selects \f[CR]always\f[R],
\f[CR]never\f[R], or \f[CR]auto\f[R] for the utility that follows, and
\f[CR]diff\f[R] and \f[CR]grep\f[R] also take \f[CR]\-\-color\f[R].
\f[CR]tabs\f[R] and \f[CR]tput\f[R] write escape sequences wherever
their output goes.
.SS Koshka\-specific and system inspection utilities
.PP
