.SH BASH AND POSIX LANGUAGE SPECIFICATION
.SS Invocation, operands, and compatibility startup
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Input source precedence\f[R] \- Evaluated in strict order:
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] (stdin), \f[CR]\-c\f[R] (inline command strings),
\f[CR]script\f[R] operand (where \f[CR]\-\f[R] selects stdin; a
directory fails with \f[CR]126\f[R], an unopenable file fails with
\f[CR]127\f[R]), \f[CR]\-i\f[R] or terminal stdin (interactive editor),
or non\-interactive stdin.
Selecting multiple sources emits a warning and uses the
highest\-priority source, except \f[CR]\-s \-i\f[R] on a terminal starts
the interactive editor.
Passing \f[CR]\-i\f[R] without a terminal fails after startup files
complete.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Positional parameter assignment (\f[CB]$0\f[B] and
\f[CB]$1..$N\f[B])\f[R] \- For scripts, \f[CR]$0\f[R] is the script path
and remaining operands become \f[CR]$1..$N\f[R].
For \f[CR]\-c\f[R], the first operand after the command string(s)
becomes \f[CR]$0\f[R] and subsequent operands become \f[CR]$1..$N\f[R].
For \f[CR]\-s\f[R] and interactive sessions, \f[CR]$0\f[R] remains the
shell invocation name and all operands become \f[CR]$1..$N\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Initial mood and login operation\f[R] \- The invocation basename
selects the initial mood (\f[CR]sh\f[R]/\f[CR]dash\f[R] select
\f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R]/\f[CR]rbash\f[R] select \f[CR]bash\f[R];
\f[CR]rbash\f[R] also requests restricted mode).
A leading dash in argument zero or \f[CR]\-l\f[R] selects login
operation.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_FLAGS\f[B]\f[R] \- Supplies options before the command
line, which override the same option from it.
Input\-selecting and mode flags (\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rcfile\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-init\-file\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[R]),
\f[CR]\-\-\f[R], and operands are dropped (see Koshka variables).
A secure execution removes it.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Invocation failure and rescue mode\f[R] \- When \f[CR]$0\f[R]
begins with \f[CR]\-\f[R] (login shell) and option parsing fails, the
shell warns and enters \f[B]rescue mode\f[R] (skipping all settings and
startup files).
A failing \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R] is dropped and the command\-line options
and input source are kept.
A failing command line in a login shell retains only \f[CR]\-r\f[R] and
reads commands from stdin (interactively on a terminal).
All other invalid invocations, including an unknown value of
\f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-init\-moods\f[R], \f[CR]\-o\f[R], or \f[CR]\-O\f[R], exit with
status \f[CR]2\f[R].
.PP
.TS
