.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rcfile\f[R] applies only to an interactive non\-login shell.
On Windows these files keep their POSIX paths.
Skipping rules are under Startup and configuration file loading.
.SS Lexical grammar, reserved words, and quoting
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Lexical tokens\f[R] \- \f[B]Blanks\f[R] are spaces and tabs.
\f[B]Metacharacters\f[R] (\f[CR]space\f[R], \f[CR]tab\f[R],
\f[CR]newline\f[R], \f[CR]|\f[R], \f[CR]&\f[R], \f[CR];\f[R],
\f[CR](\f[R], \f[CR])\f[R], \f[CR]<\f[R], \f[CR]>\f[R]) separate
unquoted words.
\f[B]Control operators\f[R] are \f[CR]newline\f[R], \f[CR]||\f[R],
\f[CR]&&\f[R], \f[CR]&\f[R], \f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR];;\f[R], \f[CR];&\f[R],
\f[CR];;&\f[R], \f[CR]|\f[R], \f[CR]|&\f[R], \f[CR](\f[R], and
\f[CR])\f[R].
\f[B]Identifiers\f[R] consist of ASCII letters, digits, and
\f[CR]_\f[R], not starting with a digit; variables and, in most
contexts, functions are identifiers.
A \f[B]word\f[R] (also a token) is a sequence of characters that the
shell treats as one unit.
\f[B]Comments\f[R] begin with an unquoted \f[CR]#\f[R] at the start of a
word and last to the end of the line (always enabled in scripts;
governed by \f[CR]interactive_comments\f[R], default \f[CR]on\f[R], in
interactive shells); a \f[CR]#\f[R] inside a word, as in \f[CR]a#b\f[R],
is an ordinary character.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Reserved words\f[R] \- Recognized only when unquoted as the first
word of a command, the third word of \f[CR]case\f[R] or \f[CR]for\f[R],
or immediately following another command\-list\-starting reserved word:
\f[CR]!\f[R] \f[CR]case\f[R] \f[CR]coproc\f[R] \f[CR]do\f[R]
\f[CR]done\f[R] \f[CR]elif\f[R] \f[CR]else\f[R] \f[CR]esac\f[R]
\f[CR]fi\f[R] \f[CR]for\f[R] \f[CR]function\f[R] \f[CR]if\f[R]
\f[CR]in\f[R] \f[CR]select\f[R] \f[CR]then\f[R] \f[CR]time\f[R]
\f[CR]until\f[R] \f[CR]while\f[R] \f[CR]{\f[R] \f[CR]}\f[R]
\f[CR][[\f[R] \f[CR]]]\f[R].
\f[CR]select\f[R] is reserved only in \f[CR]bash\f[R] and
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R].
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood does not reserve \f[CR]coproc\f[R],
\f[CR]select\f[R], or \f[CR]time\f[R] (\f[CR]time\f[R] is an ordinary
command found through \f[CR]PATH\f[R]), and rejects \f[CR][[\f[R] as a
fatal dialect error.
A reserved word in any other position is an ordinary word
(\f[CR]echo if then fi\f[R] prints its arguments).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Quoting mechanisms\f[R]:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Backslash (\f[CB]\[rs]\f[B])\f[R] \- Preserves the following
character, or joins lines before a newline (a trailing \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R]
at EOF is a syntax error in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and literal in compatibility
moods; on Windows, unquoted \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R] in native paths acts as an
escape and must be quoted or doubled).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Single quotes (\f[CB]\[aq]...\[aq]\f[B])\f[R] \- Preserve all
characters literally; cannot contain \f[CR]\[aq]\f[R] (a backslash does
not escape it).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Double quotes (\f[CB]\[dq]...\[dq]\f[B])\f[R] \- Enable
\f[CR]$\f[R], \f[CR]\[ga]\f[R], and \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R] (where
\f[CR]\[rs]\f[R] escapes only \f[CR]$\f[R], \f[CR]\[ga]\f[R],
\f[CR]\[dq]\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R], or \f[CR]newline\f[R]), preserving
field boundaries for \f[CR]\[dq]$\[at]\[dq]\f[R] and
\f[CR]\[dq]${a[\[at]]}\[dq]\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ANSI\-C quoting (\f[CB]$\[aq]...\[aq]\f[B])\f[R] \- Expands
\f[CR]\[rs]a\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]b\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]e\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]E\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]f\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]n\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]r\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]t\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]v\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]\[rs]\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]\[aq]\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]\[dq]\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]?\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]xH\f[R]/\f[CR]\[rs]xHH\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]NNN\f[R] (octal),
\f[CR]\[rs]uHHHH\f[R]/\f[CR]\[rs]UHHHHHHHH\f[R] (UTF\-8), and
\f[CR]\[rs]cX\f[R] (control byte), terminating at any \f[CR]NUL\f[R]
byte.
Any other escape stays as written, with its backslash.
Unrecognized in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, which keeps the \f[CR]$\f[R] as
an ordinary character.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Locale translation (\f[CB]$\[dq]...\[dq]\f[B])\f[R] \- Behaves as
double quotes (unrecognized in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, which keeps the
\f[CR]$\f[R] as an ordinary character).
.RE
.SS Shell grammar constructs
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood enforces \f[CR]dash\f[R] compatibility: it
rejects \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R], C\-style \f[CR]for (( ))\f[R], \f[CR]|&\f[R],
and \f[CR]<<<\f[R] at parse time; parses \f[CR]&>\f[R] as \f[CR]&\f[R]
followed by \f[CR]>\f[R]; treats \f[CR]$\[aq]...\[aq]\f[R],
\f[CR]$\[dq]...\[dq]\f[R], brace expansion, \f[CR](( ))\f[R],
\f[CR]$[ ]\f[R], \f[CR]${ cmd; }\f[R], \f[CR]${v\[at]op}\f[R], and
quotes inside \f[CR]$(( ))\f[R] as literal/standard tokens; accepts
\f[CR]<(...)\f[R], \f[CR]function\f[R], and \f[CR]a[i]=v\f[R]; and
evaluates compound array assignments \f[CR]a=(x y)\f[R] as empty scalar
assignments.
.PP
.TS
