T{
Construct
T}@T{
Syntax and execution semantics across moods
T}
_
T{
Simple commands
T}@T{
\f[CR][var=val ...] [word | redirection ...]\f[R].
All prefix assignment values expand \f[I]before\f[R] any assignment
binds (in \f[CR]a=7 b=$a cmd\f[R], \f[CR]b\f[R] receives the prior value
of \f[CR]a\f[R]).
Prefix assignments apply to the environment of that command only
(special builtins keep them in the shell in \f[CR]kosh\f[R],
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and \f[CR]sh\f[R] but not in \f[CR]bash\f[R];
functions never keep them).
Assignments without a command word apply in the shell.
Exit status is \f[CR]128 + signo\f[R] if killed by a signal.
T}
T{
Pipelines
T}@T{
\f[CR][time [\-p] [\-R]] [!] cmd1 [ { | | |& } cmd2 ... ]\f[R].
\f[CR]|&\f[R] pipes both stdout and stderr (\f[CR]2>&1 |\f[R]).
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], a builtin in the final pipeline
stage executes in the current shell process (\f[CR]echo 5 | read x\f[R]
sets \f[CR]x\f[R]); in \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], the
final stage runs in the current shell only when \f[CR]lastpipe=on\f[R]
and job control (\f[CR]monitor\f[R]) is \f[CR]off\f[R].
Every other command, including a function or compound command in the
last stage in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], runs in its own
process.
Empty pipeline stages (\f[CR]p=1 | cat\f[R]) are errors in
\f[CR]kosh\f[R], and compatibility moods apply the stage\[aq]s
redirections and give it status \f[CR]0\f[R].
Status is the last stage\[aq]s status, or the rightmost failing stage
under \f[CR]pipefail\f[R], negated by \f[CR]!\f[R], with all stage
statuses recorded in \f[CR]PIPESTATUS[\[at]]\f[R].
\f[CR]time\f[R] reports real/user/sys time and follows
\f[CR]TIMEFORMAT\f[R] (see Parameters); it prints nothing without a
pipeline.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood adds a CPU row and, when reported and nonzero,
a peak RSS row.
\f[CR]\-R\f[R] adds the RSS row in any format, and \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
selects the POSIX format without the CPU row.
T}
T{
Lists (\f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR]&\f[R], \f[CR]&&\f[R], \f[CR]||\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]&&\f[R] and \f[CR]||\f[R] share equal left\-associative precedence
and bind tighter than \f[CR];\f[R] and \f[CR]&\f[R], which share equal
precedence.
\f[CR]a && b\f[R] runs \f[CR]b\f[R] only when \f[CR]a\f[R] returns
\f[CR]0\f[R], and \f[CR]a || b\f[R] runs \f[CR]b\f[R] only when
\f[CR]a\f[R] returns nonzero; the status of an and\-or list is that of
the last command that ran.
A list ended by \f[CR];\f[R] or a newline runs in the foreground.
Trailing \f[CR]&\f[R] runs the entire preceding and\-or list
asynchronously in a background subshell (\f[CR]a && b || c &\f[R] is one
job whose variable and directory changes stay in the job; status
\f[CR]0\f[R], PID in \f[CR]$!\f[R]).
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R], words expand \f[I]before\f[R] spawning a background
simple command; in compatibility moods, the process starts before words,
assignments, and redirections expand, so an expansion error fails the
job (status \f[CR]127\f[R] for an unset parameter or
\f[CR]${name?word}\f[R] and \f[CR]1\f[R] for a failed redirection in
Bash moods, \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
Subshell \f[CR](list)\f[R] & Group \f[CR]{ list; }\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR](list)\f[R] executes in an isolated subshell environment;
\f[CR]{ list; }\f[R] executes in the current shell and requires a
trailing \f[CR];\f[R] or newline before \f[CR]}\f[R] (the braces are
reserved words and must be separate words).
Subshell changes to variables, the working directory, options, and traps
do not reach the shell.
The status is that of the list, and redirections after a compound
command apply to the whole command.
T}
T{
Arithmetic \f[CR]((expr))\f[R] & Conditional \f[CR][[ expr ]]\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]((expr))\f[R] returns \f[CR]0\f[R] if \f[CR]expr\f[R] evaluates
nonzero, and \f[CR]1\f[R] if zero or empty; the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood
treats \f[CR]((\f[R] as two subshell openings.
\f[CR][[ expr ]]\f[R] disables word splitting and globbing on operands,
returning \f[CR]0\f[R], \f[CR]1\f[R], or \f[CR]2\f[R] when the
expression cannot be evaluated.
T}
T{
Loops (\f[CR]for\f[R], \f[CR]select\f[R], \f[CR]while\f[R],
\f[CR]until\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]for name [in word ...]; do list; done\f[R] (requires
\f[CR]do...done\f[R], rejecting \f[CR]{...}\f[R]; without \f[CR]in\f[R],
iterates over the positional parameters).
\f[CR]for (( expr1; expr2; expr3 )); do list; done\f[R] evaluates
\f[CR]expr1\f[R], then runs the list and evaluates \f[CR]expr3\f[R]
while \f[CR]expr2\f[R] is nonzero, and treats empty expressions as
\f[CR]1\f[R] (a dialect error in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
\f[CR]select name [in word ...]; do list; done\f[R]
(\f[CR]bash\f[R]/\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] only) prints the expanded words
as a numbered menu on stderr, prints \f[CR]PS3\f[R], and reads a line: a
word number sets \f[CR]name\f[R] to that word, any other line sets
\f[CR]name\f[R] to empty, \f[CR]REPLY\f[R] holds the raw line, and an
empty line prints the menu again.
The body runs after each nonempty line until a \f[CR]break\f[R] or the
end of input.
\f[CR]while\f[R] loops while the condition returns \f[CR]0\f[R] and
\f[CR]until\f[R] while it returns nonzero.
The status of a loop is that of the last list or body that ran, or
\f[CR]0\f[R] when none ran.
T}
T{
Pattern branch \f[CR]case\f[R] & Conditional \f[CR]if\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]case word in [(] pat [| pat]...) list ;;|;&|;;& esac\f[R] where
\f[CR];;\f[R] stops, \f[CR];&\f[R] falls through without testing the
next pattern, and \f[CR];;&\f[R] tests subsequent patterns (every mood
accepts the three terminators).
\f[CR]if list; then list; [elif list; then list;]... [else list;] fi\f[R]
runs the \f[CR]then\f[R] list of the first condition returning
\f[CR]0\f[R], or the \f[CR]else\f[R] list.
The status of \f[CR]case\f[R] and \f[CR]if\f[R] is that of the last list
that ran, or \f[CR]0\f[R] when no pattern matched or no branch ran.
T}
T{
Coprocess \f[CR]coproc\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]coproc [NAME] command [redirections]\f[R] (unsupported in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]).
Spawns \f[CR]command\f[R] asynchronously with two pipes (> fd
\f[CR]9\f[R], closed on \f[CR]exec\f[R] and not inherited by subshells),
storing read/write descriptors in \f[CR]NAME[0]\f[R] /
\f[CR]NAME[1]\f[R] (\f[CR]COPROC\f[R] default; \f[CR]NAME\f[R] is
recognized only before a compound command) and PID in
\f[CR]NAME_PID\f[R] and \f[CR]$!\f[R].
The launch returns \f[CR]0\f[R], and the shell closes the descriptors
and unsets \f[CR]NAME\f[R] and \f[CR]NAME_PID\f[R] when the coprocess
finishes.
T}
T{
Functions \f[CR]fname()\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]fname () command [redirections]\f[R] or
\f[CR]function fname [()] command [redirections]\f[R].
Stores \f[CR]command\f[R] (any simple or compound command, as in
\f[CR]f() echo hi\f[R]) as the body, returns \f[CR]0\f[R], and attaches
redirections to every invocation.
A later definition replaces the body unless the function is read\-only
(\f[CR]readonly \-f\f[R]).
Any unquoted word (\f[CR]f.g\f[R], \f[CR]f\-g\f[R]) or quoted word can
be the function being defined; a reserved word after \f[CR]function\f[R]
is an error, and a word built by an expansion defines nothing.
Variables are global unless declared \f[CR]local\f[R]
(\f[CR]local var\f[R] without \f[CR]=val\f[R] inherits the outer value
in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], and starts unset in Bash moods).
Nesting caps at \f[CR]900\f[R] function calls and \f[CR]400\f[R] sourced
files, and the caps shrink when the stack runs short (see Functions).
T}
.TE
.SS Parameters, variables, and arrays
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Assignment evaluation\f[R] \- \f[CR]name=value\f[R] and
\f[CR]name+=value\f[R] undergo tilde expansion (after \f[CR]=\f[R] and
after any \f[CR]:\f[R]), parameter/command/arithmetic expansion, and
quote removal, without word splitting or globbing.
\f[CR]name+=value\f[R] appends to a string, adds to an integer, and
appends elements to an array.
\f[CR]declare\f[R] and \f[CR]local\f[R] give variables attributes, such
as integer, read\-only, exported, or a case conversion (see Standard
shell builtins reference).
A variable is set when it has been assigned a value, which may be empty,
and stays set until it is unset.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Special parameters\f[R]:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]$*\f[R] and \f[CR]$\[at]\f[R]: Positional parameters from
\f[CR]$1\f[R].
Unquoted, both split on \f[CR]IFS\f[R].
\f[CR]\[dq]$*\[dq]\f[R] joins with the first byte of \f[CR]IFS\f[R];
\f[CR]\[dq]$\[at]\[dq]\f[R] expands to separate words.
When joined into a single scalar (such as \f[CR]v=$\[at]\f[R]),
\f[CR]$\[at]\f[R] joins with the first byte of \f[CR]IFS\f[R] in
\f[CR]sh\f[R] and with a space in Bash/\f[CR]kosh\f[R] moods.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]$\[at]\f[R] and \f[CR]$*\f[R] are
always treated as set inside \f[CR]${\[at]\-d}\f[R] and
\f[CR]${\[at]+p}\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Positional parameters are the arguments of the shell, a function, or a
sourced file, numbered from \f[CR]1\f[R].
A parameter above \f[CR]9\f[R] needs braces: \f[CR]$10\f[R] is
\f[CR]$1\f[R] followed by \f[CR]0\f[R], and \f[CR]${10}\f[R] is the
tenth parameter.
\f[CR]set \-\-\f[R] replaces them and \f[CR]shift\f[R] discards the
first ones.
They cannot be assigned directly, and \f[CR]${\[at]:=x}\f[R] is an
error.
\f[CR]${\[at]:offset:length}\f[R] selects parameters, and an offset of
\f[CR]0\f[R] includes \f[CR]$0\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]$#\f[R] (parameter count), \f[CR]$?\f[R] (last foreground exit
status), \f[CR]$\-\f[R] (active option flags, including \f[CR]h\f[R] and
\f[CR]B\f[R] by default, \f[CR]c\f[R] for \f[CR]\-c\f[R], and
\f[CR]u\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R]), \f[CR]$$\f[R] (shell PID, preserved in
subshells), \f[CR]$!\f[R] (last background/coproc/process\-substitution
PID), \f[CR]$0\f[R] (shell/script name, unchanged inside a function),
and \f[CR]$_\f[R] (final argument of previous simple command; empty at
startup, and unchanged by an assignment with no command and by
\f[CR][[ ]]\f[R]).
\f[CR]$!\f[R] is empty before the first background command.
\f[CR]$?\f[R] also reports the status of the last command substitution.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Indexed and associative arrays\f[R] \- Indexed arrays
(\f[CR]a=(x y)\f[R], \f[CR]a=([2]=c)\f[R], \f[CR]a[i]=v\f[R],
\f[CR]a+=(w)\f[R]) support sparse non\-contiguous integer subscripts
(\f[CR]${a[i]}\f[R], where negative indices count from the end, and
scalar \f[CR]$a\f[R] is \f[CR]${a[0]}\f[R]).
Associative arrays (\f[CR]declare \-A m\f[R], \f[CR]m[key]=v\f[R])
preserve insertion order when listing keys via \f[CR]${!m[\[at]]}\f[R].
Under \f[CR]set \-u\f[R], \f[CR]${a[\[at]]}\f[R] and \f[CR]${a[*]}\f[R]
on an unset array expand cleanly to nothing, whereas \f[CR]$a\f[R] and
\f[CR]${a[0]}\f[R] trigger an unbound variable error.
Exporting an array (\f[CR]export a\f[R] / \f[CR]declare \-x a\f[R])
records the \f[CR]\-x\f[R] attribute in \f[CR]declare \-p\f[R] without
passing the array into child process environments;
\f[CR]declare \-p\f[R] prints the attribute as \f[CR]\-ax\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-Ax\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Array details\f[R] \- Indexed arrays have integer indices from
\f[CR]0\f[R] that may be missing (\f[CR]a[5]=z\f[R] on a two\-element
array leaves \f[CR]0\f[R], \f[CR]1\f[R], \f[CR]5\f[R]),
\f[CR]a=([2]=c [0]=a)\f[R] assigns explicit indices, and
\f[CR]${a[i]}\f[R] takes an arithmetic subscript.
Associative arrays map string keys to values (\f[CR]m[1+1]\f[R] holds
the key \f[CR]1+1\f[R]).
\f[CR]${a[\[at]]}\f[R] and \f[CR]${a[*]}\f[R] list elements,
\f[CR]${#a[\[at]]}\f[R] counts them, and \f[CR]${!a[\[at]]}\f[R] lists
the set indices or keys.
\f[CR]\[dq]${a[\[at]]}\[dq]\f[R] keeps one field per element,
\f[CR]\[dq]${a[\[at]]\[at]A}\[dq]\f[R] yields the words of the
declaration, \f[CR]\[dq]${a[\[at]]\[at]K}\[dq]\f[R] yields keys and
values, and \f[CR]\[dq]${a[*]}\[dq]\f[R] joins with the first byte of
\f[CR]IFS\f[R]; unquoted \f[CR]${a[\[at]]}\f[R] and \f[CR]${a[*]}\f[R]
join the same way and split the result.
\f[CR]unset \[aq]a[i]\[aq]\f[R] and \f[CR]unset \[aq]m[key]\[aq]\f[R]
remove one element.
Associative keys list in first\-assignment order, which Bash can list
differently, so a script that depends on the order should sort the keys.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, a subscript assignment creates an element, a
compound assignment assigns an empty string, and \f[CR]unset\f[R]
rejects a subscript.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Shell\-maintained and configuration variables\f[R] \- The tables
below list these variables in addition to the Koshka variables.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and Bash moods maintain the first table, and the
second table lists the variables that affect the shell.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood sets only \f[CR]PIPESTATUS\f[R],
\f[CR]LINENO\f[R], \f[CR]OPTIND\f[R], \f[CR]PPID\f[R], \f[CR]SHLVL\f[R],
\f[CR]PWD\f[R], \f[CR]OLDPWD\f[R], \f[CR]IFS\f[R], \f[CR]PS2\f[R], and
\f[CR]PS4\f[R] from them.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Maintained variable assignment\f[R] \- An assignment to a
maintained variable, such as \f[CR]BASHPID\f[R] or \f[CR]HISTCMD\f[R],
is discarded unless the entry states otherwise.
The shell ignores an inherited environment value of such a variable, and
children receive that value unchanged.
.SS Shell\-maintained variables
.PP
.TS
