T{
Expansion syntax
T}@T{
Evaluation semantics and mood constraints
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]${name:\-word}\f[R], \f[CR]${name:=word}\f[R],
\f[CR]${name:?word}\f[R], \f[CR]${name:+word}\f[R]
T}@T{
Default, assign\-default, error\-if\-unset, and alternate\-value forms
(with \f[CR]:\f[R] the test covers unset and empty, without it only
unset; \f[CR]word\f[R] expands only when used).
\f[CR]:=\f[R] cannot assign positional or special parameters, and
\f[CR]:?\f[R] prints an error with \f[CR]word\f[R] and exits a
non\-interactive shell.
Inside double quotes or here\-documents, \f[CR]word\f[R] of the
\f[CR]\-\f[R], \f[CR]+\f[R], and \f[CR]=\f[R] forms follows
double\-quote rules (\f[CR]\[dq]${v:\-\[aq]a\[aq]}\[dq]\f[R] retains
\f[CR]\[aq]a\[aq]\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]${#name}\f[R], \f[CR]${name:offset[:length]}\f[R]
T}@T{
Character length (\f[CR]${#*}\f[R], \f[CR]${#\[at]}\f[R] give the
positional parameter count) and substring/array slice (negative
\f[CR]offset\f[R] counts from the end and needs a leading space as in
\f[CR]${v: \-3}\f[R] to differ from \f[CR]:\-\f[R]; negative
\f[CR]length\f[R] excludes that many characters from the end; on an
indexed array \f[CR]${a[\[at]]:offset:length}\f[R] starts at the first
element with index at least \f[CR]offset\f[R]).
Braces are required for a positional parameter above \f[CR]9\f[R], an
array element, or a name followed by a character that could continue it.
T}
T{
\f[CR]${name#pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${name##pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${name%pat}\f[R],
\f[CR]${name%%pat}\f[R]
T}@T{
Strip shortest/longest matching prefix (\f[CR]#\f[R]/\f[CR]##\f[R]) or
suffix (\f[CR]%\f[R]/\f[CR]%%\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]${name/pat/str}\f[R], \f[CR]${name//pat/str}\f[R],
\f[CR]${name/#pat/str}\f[R], \f[CR]${name/%pat/str}\f[R]
T}@T{
Replace first, all (\f[CR]//\f[R]), prefix (\f[CR]/#\f[R]), or suffix
(\f[CR]/%\f[R]) matches.
While \f[CR]patsub_replacement=on\f[R] (default), unquoted \f[CR]&\f[R]
in \f[CR]str\f[R], or one from an unquoted expansion, stands for the
matched text, and a quoted \f[CR]&\f[R] or \f[CR]\[rs]&\f[R] is literal.
With the option off, every \f[CR]&\f[R] is literal.
T}
T{
\f[CR]${name\[ha]pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${name\[ha]\[ha]pat}\f[R],
\f[CR]${name,pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${name,,pat}\f[R],
\f[CR]${name\[ti]pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${name\[ti]\[ti]pat}\f[R]
T}@T{
Convert first or all matching characters to uppercase
(\f[CR]\[ha]\f[R]), lowercase (\f[CR],\f[R]), or inverted case
(\f[CR]\[ti]\f[R]).
Without a pattern, every character matches.
T}
T{
\f[CR]${name\[at]op}\f[R]
T}@T{
Value transformations (unsupported in \f[CR]sh\f[R]): \f[CR]Q\f[R]
(shell\-quoted), \f[CR]E\f[R] (\f[CR]$\[aq]...\[aq]\f[R] escapes),
\f[CR]P\f[R] (prompt expansion), \f[CR]A\f[R] (declaration statement),
\f[CR]a\f[R] (attribute flags), \f[CR]U\f[R]/\f[CR]L\f[R]/\f[CR]u\f[R]
(case conversion), \f[CR]K\f[R]/\f[CR]k\f[R] (array keys and values);
\f[CR]U\f[R]/\f[CR]L\f[R] convert to upper/lower case and \f[CR]u\f[R]
uppercases the first character.
Any other operator on a set parameter is a bad substitution.
T}
T{
\f[CR]${!name}\f[R], \f[CR]${!prefix*}\f[R], \f[CR]${!prefix\[at]}\f[R],
\f[CR]${!name[\[at]]}\f[R], \f[CR]${!name[*]}\f[R]
T}@T{
Indirect variable lookup (or nameref target name for
\f[CR]declare \-n\f[R]), variable names matching \f[CR]prefix\f[R], and
array indices/keys.
For a name reference \f[CR]${!name}\f[R] gives the target identifier,
and an unset or empty \f[CR]name\f[R] gives an empty value.
T}
T{
\f[CR]$(cmd)\f[R], \f[CR]\[ga]cmd\[ga]\f[R], \f[CR]$(< file)\f[R]
T}@T{
Subshell command substitution (strips trailing newlines; inside
backquotes a backslash keeps its meaning only before \f[CR]$\f[R],
\f[CR]\[ga]\f[R], or \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R]; \f[CR]$(< file)\f[R] expands to
the contents of \f[CR]file\f[R] without running a command;
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] preserves \f[CR]NUL\f[R] bytes while compatibility moods
drop them).
Runs in a subshell, leaving shell state, options, directory stack,
working directory, and creation mask unchanged.
A syntax error inside a substitution is reported at its place in the
outer source, and the script or command string holding it does not run.
Nesting deeper than \f[CR]64\f[R] levels is a syntax error.
T}
T{
\f[CR]${ cmd; }\f[R], \f[CR]${| cmd; }\f[R]
T}@T{
In\-process function substitutions (supported in \f[CR]kosh\f[R],
\f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R]): \f[CR]${ cmd; }\f[R] captures
stdout and \f[CR]${| cmd; }\f[R] expands to \f[CR]$REPLY\f[R] without
spawning a subshell.
Variable changes in both forms remain.
T}
T{
\f[CR]$((expr))\f[R], \f[CR]$[expr]\f[R]
T}@T{
Arithmetic expansion, evaluated as described under Arithmetic evaluation
(the expression first undergoes parameter expansion, command
substitution, and quote removal; empty \f[CR]$(( ))\f[R] yields
\f[CR]0\f[R]; \f[CR]$[expr]\f[R] is an obsolete spelling outside
\f[CR]sh\f[R], where it stays literal).
T}
T{
Non\-printing values
T}@T{
A value or key with a byte that does not print in the locale (control
byte, invalid UTF\-8) takes the \f[CR]$\[aq]...\[aq]\f[R] form in
\f[CR]${v\[at]Q}\f[R], \f[CR]${v\[at]A}\f[R],
\f[CR]${a[\[at]]\[at]K}\f[R], \f[CR]printf %q\f[R],
\f[CR]declare \-p\f[R], and \f[CR]export \-p\f[R].
T}
.TE
.SS Pathname globbing and pattern matching
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Pathname expansion\f[R] \- After field splitting, an unquoted word
holding \f[CR]*\f[R], \f[CR]?\f[R], or \f[CR][\f[R] is a pattern
replaced by the sorted matching pathnames.
A leading dot and every slash must match explicitly, and \f[CR].\f[R]
and \f[CR]..\f[R] are never results.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Options: \f[CR]noglob\f[R] disables expansion, \f[CR]dotglob\f[R] lets
\f[CR]*\f[R] and \f[CR]?\f[R] match a leading dot, \f[CR]nocaseglob\f[R]
ignores case, \f[CR]globstar\f[R] makes \f[CR]**\f[R] match any number
of directories (\f[CR]**/\f[R] matches directories only).
.IP \[bu] 2
Sort order is byte value in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] and
\f[CR]LC_COLLATE\f[R] order in Bash moods.
.IP \[bu] 2
A pattern with no matches is a fatal error in \f[CR]kosh\f[R], stays
literal in compatibility moods by default, expands to nothing under
\f[CR]nullglob\f[R], and errors under \f[CR]failglob\f[R].
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]GLOBIGNORE\f[B]\f[R] \- A nonempty value holds
colon\-separated patterns, and a result matching one is removed.
A pattern whose results are all removed follows the no\-match rules.
Assigning a nonempty value enables \f[CR]dotglob\f[R], and unsetting it
disables \f[CR]dotglob\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Extended globs (\f[CB]extglob\f[B])\f[R] \- Enabled by default in
\f[CR]kosh\f[R], always enabled inside \f[CR][[ == ]]\f[R] and
\f[CR][[ != ]]\f[R], and controlled by \f[CR]shopt \-s extglob\f[R]
elsewhere.
While on, \f[CR]?(list)\f[R], \f[CR]*(list)\f[R], \f[CR]+(list)\f[R],
\f[CR]\[at](list)\f[R], and \f[CR]!(list)\f[R] match zero or one, zero
or more, one or more, exactly one, or anything except one of the
\f[CR]\[rs]|\f[R]\-separated patterns.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Pattern syntax\f[R] \- Used by pathname expansion, \f[CR]case\f[R],
the \f[CR]==\f[R] and \f[CR]!=\f[R] operators of \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R], and
the pattern forms of parameter expansion.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]*\f[R] matches any string, \f[CR]?\f[R] one character,
\f[CR][...]\f[R] one character from a set (ranges such as
\f[CR]a\-z\f[R]; classes \f[CR][:alnum:]\f[R], \f[CR][:alpha:]\f[R],
\f[CR][:blank:]\f[R], \f[CR][:cntrl:]\f[R], \f[CR][:digit:]\f[R],
\f[CR][:graph:]\f[R], \f[CR][:lower:]\f[R], \f[CR][:print:]\f[R],
\f[CR][:punct:]\f[R], \f[CR][:space:]\f[R], \f[CR][:upper:]\f[R],
\f[CR][:xdigit:]\f[R]; leading \f[CR]!\f[R] or \f[CR]\[ha]\f[R] negates;
\f[CR]]\f[R] first is literal).
.IP \[bu] 2
Equivalence classes, collating symbols, and reversed ranges match
nothing.
An unclosed \f[CR][\f[R] is literal.
A backslash or quotes make a character literal.
.IP \[bu] 2
Outside pathname expansion \f[CR]*\f[R] and \f[CR]?\f[R] also match a
slash and a leading dot.
\f[CR]nocasematch\f[R] makes \f[CR]case\f[R], \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R], and
\f[CR]=\[ti]\f[R] ignore case.
The pattern of \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R] always accepts the extended forms.
.RE
.SS Redirections
Redirections open, duplicate, or close descriptors for a command and
process left to right.
A number before the operator selects the descriptor, and
\f[CR]{var}\f[R] before it allocates a descriptor numbered \f[CR]10\f[R]
or above and stores the number in \f[CR]var\f[R] (an array element is
allowed; the number stays in \f[CR]var\f[R] after the descriptor
closes).
.PP
.TS
