Operator
T}@T{
Semantics
T}
_
T{
\f[CR][n]<word\f[R]
T}@T{
Opens \f[CR]word\f[R] for reading on \f[CR]n\f[R] (default
\f[CR]0\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]>word\f[R], \f[CR][n]>|word\f[R]
T}@T{
Opens \f[CR]word\f[R] for writing on \f[CR]n\f[R] (default
\f[CR]1\f[R]), creating or truncating it; \f[CR]>\f[R] respects
\f[CR]noclobber\f[R] (refuses an existing regular file) and
\f[CR]>|\f[R] overrides it.
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]>>word\f[R]
T}@T{
Appends.
Inside a loop body the file opens once for the whole loop, so writes
continue to the original file after it is renamed or removed.
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<>word\f[R]
T}@T{
Opens for reading and writing on \f[CR]n\f[R] (default \f[CR]0\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]&>word\f[R], \f[CR]>&word\f[R], \f[CR]&>>word\f[R]
T}@T{
Redirects standard output and standard error to a file (\f[CR]&>\f[R]
and \f[CR]&>>\f[R] always open a file).
Rejected in \f[CR]sh\f[R], where \f[CR]&>\f[R] parses as \f[CR]&\f[R]
then \f[CR]>\f[R] and \f[CR]>&\f[R] takes only a descriptor.
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<&m\f[R], \f[CR][n]>&m\f[R]
T}@T{
Duplicates descriptor \f[CR]m\f[R] (a word that is not a single open
descriptor is an error).
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<&\-\f[R], \f[CR][n]>&\-\f[R]
T}@T{
Closes \f[CR]n\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<&m\-\f[R], \f[CR][n]>&m\-\f[R]
T}@T{
Moves \f[CR]m\f[R] to \f[CR]n\f[R] and closes \f[CR]m\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<<word\f[R], \f[CR][n]<<\-word\f[R]
T}@T{
Here\-document (see below).
T}
T{
\f[CR][n]<<<word\f[R]
T}@T{
Here\-string: the expanded word plus a newline is the input.
Rejected in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Target expansion\f[R] \- The target undergoes the same expansions
as an argument, except that pathname expansion yielding more than one
result is not performed.
A target that expands to several words is an error, and so is an unset
variable in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Failure status\f[R] \- A failed redirection fails the command with
\f[CR]1\f[R] in the Bash moods and \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and
\f[CR]sh\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
On a special builtin or compound command it ends the script with
\f[CR]1\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
The Bash moods continue, including \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] (where Bash
itself exits).
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Expansion scope\f[R] \- In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] all
targets expand in the parent shell.
In Bash moods the redirections of a program, of a command with no
command word, and of a subshell expand in the child (a side effect such
as \f[CR]${v:=x}\f[R] does not reach the shell), and those of a builtin,
function, group, and \f[CR]exec\f[R] expand in the shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Device paths\f[R] \- \f[CR]/dev/tcp\f[R] is not emulated; a device
path opens as an ordinary file system path.
.SS Here\-documents
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Body\f[R] \- \f[CR][n]<<word\f[R] makes the lines after the
command, up to a line that is exactly \f[CR]word\f[R], the input.
A command can have several here\-documents, read in order.
An unterminated here\-document ends at the end of the input.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Quoted delimiter\f[R] \- When any part of \f[CR]word\f[R] is
quoted, the lines are literal.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Unquoted delimiter\f[R] \- The lines undergo parameter expansion,
command substitution, and arithmetic expansion.
A backslash keeps its meaning only before \f[CR]$\f[R],
\f[CR]\[ga]\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]\f[R], or a newline, and a backslash before
a newline joins lines.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]<<\-\f[B]\f[R] \- Strips leading tabs from the lines and the
terminator.
.SS Arithmetic evaluation (\f[CR]$(( ))\f[R], \f[CR](( ))\f[R], \f[CR]let\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Contexts\f[R] \- \f[CR]$(( ))\f[R], \f[CR](( ))\f[R],
\f[CR]let\f[R], the C\-style \f[CR]for\f[R] loop, array subscripts,
substring offsets, and assignments to integer variables.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Evaluation\f[R] \- Precedence follows Bash (unary minus binds
tighter than \f[CR]**\f[R]; \f[CR]**\f[R] groups from the right).
Parentheses group, comparison and logical operators yield \f[CR]1\f[R]
or \f[CR]0\f[R], and \f[CR]&&\f[R] and \f[CR]||\f[R] evaluate the right
side only when needed.
\f[CR]**=\f[R] and \f[CR]0b\f[R] binary literals are Koshka extensions.
.PP
.TS
