.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Words\f[R] \- Inside \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R] words undergo no field
splitting or pathname expansion.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]test\f[B] combination\f[R] \- \f[CR]test\f[R] and
\f[CR][\f[R] use binary \f[CR]\-a\f[R] and \f[CR]\-o\f[R] for and/or,
and the outcome depends on the number of arguments, as POSIX specifies.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Malformed \f[CB][[ ]]\f[B]\f[R] \- Fails with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
.SS Aliases, functions, and command execution
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Aliases\f[R] \- Replace the first word of a simple command with a
string, expanded when the command runs (an alias defined earlier on the
same line or in the same compound command applies at once; an alias in a
function body takes its value at each call).
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
A replacement ending with a blank also checks the next word.
Chains are followed, a word already being replaced is not replaced
again, and quoted words, words with a backslash, words produced by an
expansion, and reserved words are never aliases.
.IP \[bu] 2
Expansion requires \f[CR]expand_aliases\f[R], on in \f[CR]kosh\f[R],
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and \f[CR]sh\f[R], and in \f[CR]bash\f[R] only
in an interactive shell, not in a script (see Default option states
across moods).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]alias\f[R] and \f[CR]unalias\f[R] manage them, and
\f[CR]BASH_ALIASES\f[R] holds them.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Functions\f[R] \- Run in the current shell with their own
positional parameters (\f[CR]$0\f[R] stays the script name).
Status is the \f[CR]return\f[R] value or the status of the last command.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Variables are global unless \f[CR]local\f[R] or \f[CR]declare\f[R] makes
them local, and a local is visible to called functions.
\f[CR]local\f[R] without a value keeps the outer value visible in
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], and starts the variable unset in the
Bash moods.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]FUNCNAME\f[R], \f[CR]BASH_SOURCE\f[R], \f[CR]BASH_LINENO\f[R], and
\f[CR]BASH_COMMAND\f[R] expose the call stack.
Calls nest at most \f[CR]900\f[R] deep and sourced files \f[CR]400\f[R]
deep (less when the stack runs short); a deeper call fails with a
diagnostic.
.IP \[bu] 2
A function called from a guarded and\-or operand or a negation keeps
\f[CR]errexit\f[R] suppressed throughout its body.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[CR]RETURN\f[R] trap fires at the end of every function body in
\f[CR]kosh\f[R], for bodies that \f[CR]functrace\f[R] follows in the
Bash moods, and is rejected in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]export \-f\f[R] exports a function as \f[CR]BASH_FUNC_name%%\f[R]
for Bash children.
Koshka does not import exported functions.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Command lookup\f[R] \- A word holding a slash runs that path
directly.
Other words resolve in the order under Command execution lifecycle at
the top of this manual.
Running a command or \f[CR]hash\f[R] caches its path until
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] is assigned, \f[CR]hash \-r\f[R] runs, or a directory
change affects a relative \f[CR]PATH\f[R] entry.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
A program runs in a separate process with the exported variables as
environment.
A command not found returns \f[CR]127\f[R], a file that cannot run
returns \f[CR]126\f[R], and a path ending in a directory separator fails
with \f[CR]126\f[R] when the target is not a directory.
.IP \[bu] 2
A file without a recognized executable format runs as a shell script in
a new shell, without the variables, functions, and traps of the caller.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Directory commands\f[R] \- In an interactive shell a single
directory path in command position changes to that directory as
\f[CR]cd\f[R] would, unless a function, builtin, program, or bundled
utility of that name exists.
A word of only dots moves up one directory per dot after the first.
Controlled by \f[CR]autocd\f[R] (on in every mood, never affects
scripts).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Execution environment\f[R] \- Consists of open files, working
directory, creation mask, resource limits, traps, variables and
attributes, functions, options, aliases, directory stack, and job table.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
A subshell, command substitution, separate pipeline stage, or
asynchronous list receives a copy whose changes do not reach the shell.
Caught traps reset to defaults there, ignored traps stay ignored, and a
subshell cannot wait for a job of its parent.
.IP \[bu] 2
A program receives the open descriptors, working directory, creation
mask, resource limits, ignored signals, and exported variables.
Every descriptor the shell opened for itself is closed in the program.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Environment variables\f[R] \- At startup the shell imports its
environment and marks every variable for export.
Inherited variables and those marked with \f[CR]export\f[R] or
\f[CR]declare \-x\f[R] pass to every program, and a prefix assignment
adds a variable for one command.
Shell\-maintained values (\f[CR]SECONDS\f[R], \f[CR]IFS\f[R],
\f[CR]SHELLOPTS\f[R], the Koshka variables) are not taken from the
environment.
Names beginning with \f[CR]KOSH_INTERNAL_\f[R] are reserved.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
On Windows, names are case\-insensitive and a value holds at most
\f[CR]32,766\f[R] UTF\-16 units, so longer values reach a child
truncated.
.RE
.SS Error handling, exit statuses, signals, and job control
.SS Error termination matrix across moods
.PP
.TS
