.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Syntax errors\f[R] \- The parser reports every syntax error in one
run.
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R], analysis can also reject a script before it runs
with status \f[CR]1\f[R] (see Static analysis and runtime diagnostics).
An interactive shell rejects the line and reads the next one.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Kosh mood\f[R] \- Every arithmetic and expansion error ends the
script with \f[CR]1\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Also ends it: a failed redirection of a special builtin or compound
command (\f[CR]1\f[R]), a write error on standard output (\f[CR]1\f[R]),
an invalid regular expression (\f[CR]1\f[R]), and a malformed
\f[CR][[ ]]\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
A failure of another builtin, such as a missing alias, only sets the
status.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Bash moods\f[R] \- An arithmetic error in \f[CR]$(( ))\f[R], a
subscript, a substring offset, or an integer\-variable assignment
abandons the rest of the input line, as in Bash.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Remaining commands on the line do not run, \f[CR]$?\f[R] is \f[CR]1\f[R]
on the next line, and neither \f[CR]set \-e\f[R] nor an \f[CR]ERR\f[R]
trap acts on it.
An \f[CR]eval\f[R], sourced file, subshell, and command substitution
each count as a separate line.
.IP \[bu] 2
A glob matching nothing under \f[CR]failglob\f[R] abandons the line in
\f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R].
An interactive shell abandons the line instead of ending.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Eval, source, and containment\f[R] \- In \f[CR]sh\f[R] and
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], a syntax error in an \f[CR]eval\f[R] string or
sourced file ends a non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R]; in
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\f[R] it fails only that command with
\f[CR]2\f[R].
An error that ends a non\-interactive shell passes through
\f[CR]eval\f[R] and \f[CR]source\f[R], as in Bash and dash.
A subshell, command substitution, trap action, and startup file contain
it, and the command that started them receives the status.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Special builtins and exec\f[R] \- In \f[CR]sh\f[R], an error of a
regular builtin (missing \f[CR]cd\f[R] directory, invalid
\f[CR]test\f[R] operand) fails that command with \f[CR]2\f[R] and the
script continues.
An error of a special builtin or \f[CR]local\f[R] (\f[CR]shift\f[R] past
the last parameter, \f[CR]export\f[R] of an invalid identifier) ends a
non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] unless \f[CR]command\f[R] runs
it.
A failed \f[CR]exec\f[R] ends a non\-interactive shell in every mood.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Standard exit codes\f[R] \- A command status is \f[CR]0\f[R] to
\f[CR]255\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] success (also an empty command, a compound command that ran
no list, and an asynchronous command).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]1\f[R] general failure (false condition, failed builtin, analysis
that rejected a script).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]2\f[R] usage error of a builtin or the shell, or syntax error.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]126\f[R] found but cannot be run (directory, file without execute
permission, path ending in a separator that is not a directory).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]127\f[R] command not found, or a script operand that cannot be
opened.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]128 + n\f[R] ended by signal \f[CR]n\f[R] (\f[CR]130\f[R]
\f[CR]SIGINT\f[R], \f[CR]141\f[R] \f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R], \f[CR]143\f[R]
\f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R]).
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Shell exit status\f[R] \- Status of the last command run, or the
\f[CR]exit\f[R] operand modulo \f[CR]256\f[R].
When an expansion error ends a non\-interactive shell the status is
\f[CR]1\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
In the Bash moods an unset parameter under \f[CR]nounset\f[R],
\f[CR]${name:?}\f[R], and a bad substitution that ends the shell return
\f[CR]127\f[R] from a \f[CR]\-c\f[R] string, as in Bash, and
\f[CR]1\f[R] from a script.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Tool exit statuses\f[R] \- \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R] return \f[CR]0\f[R] when
every input succeeds (warning\-only findings included), \f[CR]1\f[R]
when an input cannot be read, parsed, formatted, or replaced or analysis
reports an error, and \f[CR]2\f[R] for an invalid option combination.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Signals and PID 1 reaper\f[R] \- The shell ignores
\f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R] (reset to default in child processes), so a producer
killed by it returns \f[CR]141\f[R].
Interactive shells also ignore \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R], \f[CR]SIGQUIT\f[R],
\f[CR]SIGTSTP\f[R], and \f[CR]SIGTTIN\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] interrupts the running command or the line being
edited and reports \f[CR]Interrupted\f[R] with status \f[CR]130\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
A shell that does not trap \f[CR]SIGHUP\f[R], and a non\-interactive
shell that does not trap \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] or \f[CR]SIGQUIT\f[R], is
terminated by the signal, so its parent sees \f[CR]128\f[R] plus the
signal number.
.IP \[bu] 2
As process 1, \f[CR]kosh\f[R] reaps orphaned children it did not start
(before each command and while it waits).
On \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] it sends \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] then
\f[CR]SIGCONT\f[R] to every other process, interrupts the running
command, waits for every remaining child, and exits with \f[CR]143\f[R].
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Signal reports and traps\f[R] \- A command ended by a signal prints
a line such as \f[CR][Process 2585: Killed, signal 9]\f[R] on standard
error, once per killed pipeline stage, and its status is \f[CR]128\f[R]
plus the signal number (\f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R] and \f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] print
no line).
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]trap\f[R] sets the action of a signal or of \f[CR]EXIT\f[R],
\f[CR]ERR\f[R], \f[CR]DEBUG\f[R], \f[CR]RETURN\f[R].
A trapped signal runs its action at the next command boundary.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[CR]CHLD\f[R] trap runs once per reaped child (under
\f[CR]set \-m\f[R] a job that stops counts too).
.IP \[bu] 2
A trapped signal that arrives while \f[CR]wait\f[R] blocks makes
\f[CR]wait\f[R] return \f[CR]128\f[R] plus the signal number, and the
job keeps running.
.IP \[bu] 2
On Windows a background process runs in its own console process group.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Jobs\f[R] \- A job is a background pipeline or list, or a stopped
foreground pipeline.
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job, and POSIX
stages share a process group.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
The final stage determines \f[CR]$!\f[R], the status, and the job
identity (under \f[CR]pipefail\f[R] the rightmost failing stage may
determine the status).
.IP \[bu] 2
A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the last, and
\f[CR]fg\f[R] and \f[CR]bg\f[R] resume every stage.
.IP \[bu] 2
Job control is on in an interactive shell, where Ctrl\-Z stops the
foreground job.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Job specs\f[R] \- \f[CR]%n\f[R] job \f[CR]n\f[R]; \f[CR]%+\f[R],
\f[CR]%%\f[R], \f[CR]%\f[R] the current job; \f[CR]%\-\f[R] the previous
job; \f[CR]%string\f[R] the job whose command begins with
\f[CR]string\f[R]; \f[CR]%?string\f[R] the job whose command contains
it.
The lowest\-numbered job wins among several matches, and a bare number
is also a job number.
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] an unquoted \f[CR]%?string\f[R] is a glob and needs
quotes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Job reports\f[R] \- \f[CR]jobs\f[R] and job notifications print one
line per job, as in Bash: job number with \f[CR]+\f[R] (current) or
\f[CR]\-\f[R] (previous), the process with \f[CR]jobs \-l\f[R], the
state, and the command.
A job ended by a signal shows its description (\f[CR]Terminated\f[R]),
and an exited job shows \f[CR]Done\f[R], or \f[CR]Exit\f[R] and its
status.
An interactive shell reports a job that finished or changed state before
the next prompt.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Remembered statuses\f[R] \- A job reported by \f[CR]wait\f[R],
\f[CR]jobs\f[R], or a notification leaves the job table, and a later
\f[CR]wait\f[R] on its process returns the remembered status.
The shell remembers the \f[CR]1024\f[R] most recent statuses, and
\f[CR]wait\f[R] without operands discards them.
A subshell, pipeline stage, asynchronous command, and coprocess cannot
wait for a job of their parent.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Hangup\f[R] \- The shell never sends \f[CR]SIGHUP\f[R] to its jobs,
so background jobs continue after it exits, and it exits even while jobs
are stopped.
\f[CR]disown\f[R] removes a job from the table.
.SS History expansion
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Scope\f[R] \- Interactive shells with \f[CR]histexpand\f[R] on
(default; \f[CR]set +H\f[R] turns it off) replace each reference before
parsing and print the replaced line before running it.
Non\-interactive shells do not expand history.
A reference is an event, an optional word designator, and optional
modifiers.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Quoting\f[R] \- Expands inside double quotes.
A single\-quoted or backslash\-escaped \f[CR]!\f[R] stays literal.
A \f[CR]!\f[R] stays literal before a blank, \f[CR]=\f[R], \f[CR](\f[R],
or a quote, after \f[CR]$\f[R], inside \f[CR][...]\f[R] or
\f[CR]${...}\f[R], and in a comment.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Errors\f[R] \- Event not found, bad word specifier, unrecognized
modifier, a substitution that finds no match (including \f[CR]:&\f[R]
before any substitution), a \f[CR]:s\f[R] without a delimiter after
\f[CR]old\f[R], and a \f[CR]\[ha]old\[ha]new\[ha]\f[R] without a
\f[CR]\[ha]\f[R] after \f[CR]old\f[R] or whose \f[CR]old\f[R] is absent
from the previous event.
The message points at the reference, and the line neither runs nor
enters the history.
.SS Event designators
.PP
.TS
