Modifier
T}@T{
Effect
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]:h\f[R], \f[CR]:t\f[R], \f[CR]:r\f[R], \f[CR]:e\f[R]
T}@T{
Removes the last path component, keeps only the last component, removes
a trailing suffix, keeps only the suffix.
T}
T{
\f[CR]:s/old/new/\f[R]
T}@T{
Replaces the first literal \f[CR]old\f[R] (another delimiter may replace
the slash, the last delimiter may be omitted at the end of the line,
\f[CR]&\f[R] in \f[CR]new\f[R] stands for \f[CR]old\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]&\f[R] is a literal \f[CR]&\f[R], an empty \f[CR]old\f[R]
reuses the last substitution or search text).
T}
T{
\f[CR]:gs\f[R], \f[CR]:as\f[R]
T}@T{
Replaces every occurrence.
T}
T{
\f[CR]:Gs\f[R]
T}@T{
Replaces the first occurrence in each word.
T}
T{
\f[CR]:&\f[R]
T}@T{
Repeats the last substitution (\f[CR]:g&\f[R], \f[CR]:a&\f[R],
\f[CR]:G&\f[R] with the matching scope).
T}
T{
\f[CR]:q\f[R], \f[CR]:x\f[R]
T}@T{
Quotes the text as one argument, or each word separately.
T}
T{
\f[CR]:p\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints the result and records it in the history without running it.
T}
.TE
.SS Standard shell builtins reference
A builtin runs inside the shell.
Special builtins are \f[CR]:\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R], \f[CR]break\f[R],
\f[CR]continue\f[R], \f[CR]eval\f[R], \f[CR]exec\f[R], \f[CR]exit\f[R],
\f[CR]export\f[R], \f[CR]readonly\f[R], \f[CR]return\f[R],
\f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]shift\f[R], \f[CR]times\f[R], \f[CR]trap\f[R],
\f[CR]unset\f[R], and (in Bash moods) \f[CR]source\f[R].
Assignments before a special builtin remain in the shell, as described
under Simple commands, and their errors follow the rules under Shell
errors.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood hides \f[CR]let\f[R] and \f[CR]time\f[R].
.PP
A builtin given \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] before its other arguments prints
its description, synopsis, and options (in the compatibility moods
\f[CR]echo\f[R] prints \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] and \f[CR]test\f[R] ignores
it, and \f[CR][ \-\-help\f[R] reports a missing \f[CR]]\f[R] in every
mood).
Unless an entry states otherwise, a builtin returns \f[CR]0\f[R] on
success, \f[CR]1\f[R] on failure, and \f[CR]2\f[R] for an unknown option
or a wrong number of operands.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood a failing regular builtin such as
\f[CR]cd\f[R], \f[CR]dirs\f[R], \f[CR]pushd\f[R], \f[CR]popd\f[R],
\f[CR]bg\f[R], \f[CR]fg\f[R], \f[CR]jobs\f[R], \f[CR]disown\f[R],
\f[CR]kill\f[R], \f[CR]umask\f[R], or \f[CR]ulimit\f[R] returns
\f[CR]2\f[R] where the other moods return \f[CR]1\f[R], and a failing
special builtin or \f[CR]local\f[R] ends a non\-interactive shell with
\f[CR]2\f[R].
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood the failures listed in the error termination
matrix end the script.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Builtin and synopsis
T}@T{
Operational semantics and mood rules
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]: [arg ...]\f[R], \f[CR]true [arg ...]\f[R],
\f[CR]false [arg ...]\f[R] 
T}@T{
Does nothing and returns \f[CR]0\f[R] for \f[CR]:\f[R] and
\f[CR]true\f[R] (arguments are expanded and ignored, \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
prints the help); \f[CR]false\f[R] returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]. [\-\-mood[=]MOOD] file [arg ...]\f[R],
\f[CR]source [\-\-mood[=]MOOD] file [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Runs \f[CR]file\f[R] in the current shell and returns the status of its
last command or the operand of a \f[CR]return\f[R] in it (arguments
become the positional parameters while it runs and are ignored in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]; \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] runs it in \f[CR]MOOD\f[R] instead of
the mood its shebang or name selects and restores the previous mood
afterwards).
With \f[CR]sourcepath\f[R] on (default), a file without a slash is
searched in \f[CR]PATH\f[R], then the current directory.
A missing or unreadable file returns \f[CR]1\f[R], and ends a
non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R] and
\f[CR]1\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R].
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R], a Bash startup file (\f[CR].bashrc\f[R],
\f[CR].bash_profile\f[R], \f[CR].bash_login\f[R],
\f[CR].bash_logout\f[R], \f[CR].profile\f[R], \f[CR]/etc/profile\f[R])
runs in \f[CR]bash\f[R] with its options and a file whose shebang
selects another shell runs in that mood, while a file without a shebang
runs in the current mood.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood returns when the file ends and keeps the
variables, functions, and aliases it defined.
Analysis does not follow such a file, a syntax error in it fails the
command without ending the shell, and the files it sources keep its
mood.
T}
T{
\f[CR][ expr ]\f[R], \f[CR]test expr\f[R]
T}@T{
Evaluates conditional expression, see Conditional expressions
(\f[CR]0\f[R] true, \f[CR]1\f[R] false or no expression, \f[CR]2\f[R]
malformed expression, missing \f[CR]]\f[R], or non\-decimal integer
operand).
Rejects \f[CR]==\f[R] with a hint to use \f[CR]=\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R]
and \f[CR]sh\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]alias [name[=val] ...]\f[R], \f[CR]unalias [\-a] name ...\f[R] 
T}@T{
Defines aliases for names with a value, prints definitions of names
without one, or prints every alias as an \f[CR]alias\f[R] command
without operands (\f[CR]alias\f[R]), and removes aliases
(\f[CR]unalias\f[R], \f[CR]\-a\f[R] every alias).
A name may hold any character except \f[CR]=\f[R].
An unknown alias or an empty name returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]bg [job]\f[R], \f[CR]fg [job]\f[R],
\f[CR]jobs [\-lnprs] [job ...]\f[R], \f[CR]disown [\-ahr] [job ...]\f[R]
\  
T}@T{
Job control builtins, see Job control: resume a stopped job in the
background (\f[CR]bg\f[R]) or foreground (\f[CR]fg\f[R], returns the
job\[aq]s status), list jobs (\f[CR]jobs\f[R]: \f[CR]\-l\f[R] adds PIDs,
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] only PIDs, \f[CR]\-r\f[R]/\f[CR]\-s\f[R] running or
stopped jobs, \f[CR]\-n\f[R] jobs changed since the last report), or
remove jobs from the job table (\f[CR]disown\f[R]:
\f[CR]\-a\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] every job,
\f[CR]\-r\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-running\f[R] running jobs,
\f[CR]\-h\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-nohup\f[R] keeps the job in the table).
\f[CR]bg\f[R], \f[CR]fg\f[R], and \f[CR]disown\f[R] act on the current
job by default.
A missing job returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]wait [\-fn] [\-p var] [pid|job ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Waits for each process or job and returns the status of the last one, or
waits for every child and returns \f[CR]0\f[R] (\f[CR]\-n\f[R] next job
to finish among the operands or all jobs, \f[CR]127\f[R] when none is
left; \f[CR]\-p var\f[R] stores the process whose status is returned or
unsets the variable; \f[CR]\-f\f[R] waits until each job terminates).
A stopped job returns \f[CR]128\f[R] plus the stop signal and
\f[CR]wait \-n\f[R] skips it.
A process that is not a child of the shell or a job that does not exist
returns \f[CR]127\f[R] with a diagnostic (\f[CR]2\f[R] for a missing job
number in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]suspend [\-f]\f[R]
T}@T{
Stops the shell until it receives \f[CR]SIGCONT\f[R].
A login shell refuses without \f[CR]\-f\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]bind [options] [keyseq:fn ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Accepts readline options (\f[CR]\-lmrvpPsq\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R],
\f[CR]\-u\f[R]) and operands without changing key bindings and returns
\f[CR]0\f[R]; see Line editing.
T}
T{
\f[CR]break [n]\f[R], \f[CR]continue [n]\f[R]
T}@T{
Leaves the innermost \f[CR]n\f[R] enclosing loops (\f[CR]break\f[R]) or
starts their next iteration (\f[CR]continue\f[R]), default \f[CR]1\f[R];
a count above the nesting depth applies to the outermost loop.
Count \f[CR]0\f[R] returns \f[CR]1\f[R] and leaves every enclosing loop
in the Bash moods, only the innermost loop in \f[CR]kosh\f[R], and ends
a non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
A non\-numeric count ends a non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in
every mood.
Outside a loop, and for a loop in a calling function, the commands leave
nothing (the Bash moods print a diagnostic).
T}
T{
\f[CR]builtin [\-\-list] [name [arg ...]]\f[R]
T}@T{
Runs builtin \f[CR]name\f[R] even when a function of that name exists (a
name that is not a builtin returns \f[CR]1\f[R]; \f[CR]\-\-list\f[R]
prints every builtin one per line; without an operand, \f[CR]kosh\f[R]
lists the builtins under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings).
T}
T{
\f[CR]command [\-pVv] name [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Runs \f[CR]name\f[R] as a builtin or program, skipping functions and
aliases, and a special builtin run this way loses its special properties
(\f[CR]\-p\f[R] searches the default \f[CR]PATH\f[R], \f[CR]\-v\f[R]
prints the path, builtin, keyword, function, or alias definition
\f[CR]name\f[R] resolves to, \f[CR]\-V\f[R] describes it in words).
With \f[CR]\-v\f[R] or \f[CR]\-V\f[R] returns \f[CR]0\f[R] when any
operand resolves and \f[CR]1\f[R] when none does (\f[CR]127\f[R] in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]).
A command that is not found returns \f[CR]127\f[R], and one that cannot
run \f[CR]126\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]type [\-aPptV] name ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Describes how each name is interpreted as a command (\f[CR]\-t\f[R]
prints \f[CR]alias\f[R], \f[CR]keyword\f[R], \f[CR]function\f[R],
\f[CR]builtin\f[R], or \f[CR]file\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R]/\f[CR]\-P\f[R]
the path of a program, \f[CR]\-a\f[R] every interpretation,
\f[CR]\-V\f[R] everything the shell holds about the word, such as a
function\[aq]s definition site and body or the description of a builtin
or utility).
The Bash moods report a function as \[dq]is a function\[dq] followed by
its body, the other moods as \[dq]is a shell function\[dq].
A name that is not found returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]127\f[R] in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]enable [\-adnps] [\-f file] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Enables builtins and bundled utilities, or disables them
(\f[CR]\-n\f[R]).
Without operands or with \f[CR]\-p\f[R] lists enabled builtins and
reachable utilities as \f[CR]enable\f[R] commands (\f[CR]\-n\f[R]
disabled ones, \f[CR]\-a\f[R] both, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] special builtins
only).
Each builtin word is separate, so disabling \f[CR]test\f[R] leaves
\f[CR][\f[R] enabled, and a utility alias such as \f[CR]egrep\f[R] is
rejected and follows its utility.
Lookup skips a disabled builtin and searches \f[CR]PATH\f[R] and the
bundled utilities.
A disabled bundled utility never resolves as a command, though the
\f[CR]koshkit\f[R] builtin still runs it, and a disabled special builtin
loses its special properties.
A subshell inherits the state, and its changes do not reach the parent.
A builtin the mood hides, such as \f[CR]let\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R], is
neither listed nor accepted.
Koshka has no loadable builtins, so \f[CR]\-f\f[R] with an operand,
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], and a name that is not a builtin or utility return
\f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]hash [\-rRt] [\-p path] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Finds each name in \f[CR]PATH\f[R] and remembers its location
(\f[CR]\-r\f[R] forgets every location, \f[CR]\-R\f[R] also searches
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] again, \f[CR]\-p path\f[R] records \f[CR]path\f[R],
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] prints the remembered location).
A name that cannot be found returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]help [\-dms] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints the help of each builtin as its \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] does
(\f[CR]\-d\f[R] short description, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] synopsis,
\f[CR]\-m\f[R] manual page); without a name, lists the builtins.
A name that is not a builtin returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]caller [n]\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints line, function, and file of call frame \f[CR]n\f[R] (\f[CR]0\f[R]
is the caller of the current function); a frame past the last returns
\f[CR]1\f[R].
Without an operand, prints line and file of the caller inside a
function, sourced file, or script file (the top level prints
\f[CR]0 NULL\f[R]) and returns \f[CR]1\f[R] in a command string or
standard input.
A non\-whole\-number operand returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and a second operand
is ignored.
T}
T{
\f[CR]cd [\-L|\-P] [dir]\f[R]
T}@T{
Changes the working directory to \f[CR]dir\f[R] (default
\f[CR]HOME\f[R]) and sets \f[CR]PWD\f[R] and \f[CR]OLDPWD\f[R];
\f[CR]\-\f[R] changes to \f[CR]OLDPWD\f[R] and prints it.
A relative \f[CR]dir\f[R] that does not begin with \f[CR].\f[R] or
\f[CR]..\f[R] is searched in each \f[CR]CDPATH\f[R] directory, and a
directory found there is printed.
A relative directory resolves against the logical \f[CR]PWD\f[R], with
\f[CR]..\f[R] removing its last component, unless \f[CR]\-P\f[R] or the
\f[CR]physical\f[R] option resolves symbolic links first (\f[CR]\-L\f[R]
keeps the logical path).
A missing directory or an unset \f[CR]HOME\f[R] or \f[CR]OLDPWD\f[R]
returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R]), and more than one
operand returns \f[CR]2\f[R].
An empty operand returns \f[CR]1\f[R] in the Bash moods and is accepted
in the other moods.
A restricted shell refuses \f[CR]cd\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]pwd [\-LPW]\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints the working directory (\f[CR]\-L\f[R] logical path, the default
unless \f[CR]physical\f[R] is on, \f[CR]\-P\f[R] path with symbolic
links resolved, \f[CR]\-W\f[R] physical path in Windows form with
forward slashes as MSYS does, and \f[CR]\-P\f[R] elsewhere).
T}
T{
\f[CR]dirs [\-clpv] [+N|\-N]\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints the current directory and the saved directory stack on one line
with \f[CR]HOME\f[R] shown as \f[CR]\[ti]\f[R] (\f[CR]\-c\f[R] clears
the stack, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] full paths, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] one entry per line,
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] entries numbered from \f[CR]0\f[R], \f[CR]+N\f[R] the Nth
entry from the top, \f[CR]\-N\f[R] the Nth from the bottom).
An entry past the end returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]pushd [\-n] [dir|+N|\-N]\f[R]
T}@T{
Pushes \f[CR]dir\f[R] onto the stack and changes to it (\f[CR]\-n\f[R]
adds \f[CR]dir\f[R] below the current directory without changing
directory).
Without an operand swaps the two top entries, and
\f[CR]+N\f[R]/\f[CR]\-N\f[R] rotates the stack so that entry
\f[CR]N\f[R] is on top.
A missing directory or an empty stack returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R]
in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]popd [\-n] [+N|\-N]\f[R]
T}@T{
Removes the top entry, or entry \f[CR]N\f[R], from the stack and changes
to the new top (\f[CR]\-n\f[R] removes the entry below the current
directory without changing directory).
An empty stack returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]compgen [options] [word]\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints the candidates the options generate that begin with
\f[CR]word\f[R], one per line.
Sources are \f[CR]\-a\f[R] aliases, \f[CR]\-b\f[R] builtins,
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] commands, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] directories, \f[CR]\-e\f[R]
exported variables, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] files, \f[CR]\-g\f[R] groups,
\f[CR]\-j\f[R] jobs, \f[CR]\-k\f[R] reserved words, \f[CR]\-s\f[R]
services, \f[CR]\-u\f[R] users, \f[CR]\-v\f[R] variables, and
\f[CR]\-A action\f[R] (\f[CR]alias\f[R], \f[CR]arrayvar\f[R],
\f[CR]binding\f[R], \f[CR]builtin\f[R], \f[CR]command\f[R],
\f[CR]directory\f[R], \f[CR]disabled\f[R], \f[CR]enabled\f[R],
\f[CR]export\f[R], \f[CR]file\f[R], \f[CR]function\f[R],
\f[CR]group\f[R], \f[CR]helptopic\f[R], \f[CR]hostname\f[R],
\f[CR]job\f[R], \f[CR]keyword\f[R], \f[CR]running\f[R],
\f[CR]service\f[R], \f[CR]setopt\f[R], \f[CR]shopt\f[R],
\f[CR]signal\f[R], \f[CR]stopped\f[R], \f[CR]user\f[R],
\f[CR]variable\f[R]).
Also takes \f[CR]\-W wordlist\f[R] (splits the expanded list),
\f[CR]\-G glob\f[R] (adds pathname matches), \f[CR]\-X pattern\f[R]
(removes matches, or keeps only matches after a leading \f[CR]!\f[R]; an
unescaped \f[CR]&\f[R] stands for the word),
\f[CR]\-P prefix\f[R]/\f[CR]\-S suffix\f[R] (added after matching and
filtering), and \f[CR]\-V array\f[R] (stores the candidates in an
indexed array and prints nothing); \f[CR]\-o\f[R], \f[CR]\-F\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-C\f[R] are accepted without effect.
No candidate returns \f[CR]1\f[R], and an unknown action returns
\f[CR]2\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]complete [options] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Registers how the arguments of each command complete from the actions of
\f[CR]compgen\f[R], \f[CR]\-G\f[R], \f[CR]\-W\f[R],
\f[CR]\-F function\f[R], \f[CR]\-C command\f[R], and \f[CR]\-o\f[R]
options (\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints specs in Bash order, \f[CR]\-r\f[R]
removes the given specs or all, \f[CR]\-D\f[R] default, \f[CR]\-E\f[R]
empty line, \f[CR]\-I\f[R] command word).
A missing spec returns \f[CR]1\f[R], and an unknown \f[CR]\-o\f[R]
option or \f[CR]\-A\f[R] action returns \f[CR]2\f[R] and registers
nothing.
See the bullets below the table.
T}
T{
\f[CR]compopt [\-o|+o opt] [\-DEI] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Enables (\f[CR]\-o option\f[R]) or disables (\f[CR]+o option\f[R]) a
\f[CR]complete \-o\f[R] option; if both forms give the same option, it
is disabled.
The change applies to each given command\[aq]s spec, to the first of the
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-E\f[R], and \f[CR]\-I\f[R] specs, or, inside a
completion function with neither, to the current completion.
Without \f[CR]\-o\f[R] or \f[CR]+o\f[R] prints one \f[CR]compopt\f[R]
line per spec.
An unknown option returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and a missing spec or a missing
target outside a completion function returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]declare [\-aAfFgilnprtux] [name[=val] ...]\f[R],
\f[CR]typeset ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Sets variables and attributes, local inside a function unless
\f[CR]\-g\f[R] (\f[CR]\-a\f[R] indexed array, \f[CR]\-A\f[R] associative
array, \f[CR]\-i\f[R] integer with arithmetic assignment,
\f[CR]\-l\f[R]/\f[CR]\-u\f[R] lowercase or uppercase conversion,
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] read\-only, \f[CR]\-x\f[R] exported, \f[CR]\-n\f[R] name
reference, \f[CR]\-t\f[R] accepted without effect).
A plus removes an attribute, except that read\-only stays.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R]/\f[CR]\-F\f[R] print functions with or without
definitions, and \f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints declarations (\f[CR]\-p\f[R] or
an attribute option without operands lists every variable with those
attributes).
Without options or operands prints nothing.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] with \f[CR]\-A\f[R] returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and a missing
variable or function or an invalid identifier returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
See the name reference bullet below the table.
T}
T{
\f[CR]local [\-aAilnprux] [name[=val] ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Makes variables local to the running function with the attributes of the
same \f[CR]declare\f[R] options (\f[CR]local \-n\f[R] passes a variable
of the caller by reference, a \f[CR]\-\f[R] operand saves the
\f[CR]set\f[R] options until the function returns, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] lists
local variables).
Outside a function returns \f[CR]1\f[R], and ends a non\-interactive
shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]readonly [\-aAfp] [name[=val] ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Marks variables read\-only after assigning a given value (\f[CR]\-p\f[R]
or no operand lists them reusably, \f[CR]\-a\f[R]/\f[CR]\-A\f[R] treat
operands as arrays, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] marks functions, or lists them with
bodies when given alone).
Assigning a read\-only variable is an error that ends a non\-interactive
shell in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]export [\-fnp] [name[=val] ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Marks variables for the environment of later commands after assigning a
given value (\f[CR]\-n\f[R] removes the mark, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] marks
functions, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] or no operand prints exported variables as
\f[CR]export\f[R] commands in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] and as
\f[CR]declare \-x\f[R] commands in the Bash moods).
An invalid identifier or a read\-only variable returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]unset [\-fnv] name ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Removes variables or array elements (\f[CR]\-v\f[R]), functions
(\f[CR]\-f\f[R]), or a name reference itself (\f[CR]\-n\f[R]).
Without an option removes a variable, or a function when no such
variable exists.
A read\-only variable returns \f[CR]1\f[R], and an unset name succeeds.
T}
T{
\f[CR]echo [\-neE] [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Joins arguments with spaces and a newline (\f[CR]\-n\f[R] omits the
newline, \f[CR]\-e\f[R] interprets backslash escapes, \f[CR]\-E\f[R]
disables them).
Escapes are interpreted by default in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] (backslashes stay
literal without \f[CR]\-e\f[R] on Windows) and in \f[CR]sh\f[R], and in
the Bash moods with \f[CR]xpg_echo\f[R].
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood accepts only \f[CR]\-n\f[R] and treats
\f[CR]\[rs]u\f[R] as an escape while \f[CR]\[rs]e\f[R] and
\f[CR]\[rs]x\f[R] stay literal.
T}
T{
\f[CR]printf [\-v var] format [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Reuses \f[CR]format\f[R] while arguments remain.
Accepts the C conversions \f[CR]d\f[R], \f[CR]i\f[R], \f[CR]o\f[R],
\f[CR]u\f[R], \f[CR]x\f[R], \f[CR]X\f[R], \f[CR]e\f[R], \f[CR]E\f[R],
\f[CR]f\f[R], \f[CR]F\f[R], \f[CR]g\f[R], \f[CR]G\f[R], \f[CR]c\f[R],
\f[CR]s\f[R] with flags, width, and precision (\f[CR]*\f[R] takes them
from an argument), plus \f[CR]%b\f[R] (backslash escapes), \f[CR]%q\f[R]
(shell\-quoted), and \f[CR]%(fmt)T\f[R] (strftime, \f[CR]\-1\f[R] for
the current time).
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] assigns the result to a variable or array element.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects \f[CR]\-v\f[R] and prints
\f[CR]%(fmt)T\f[R] as written.
An invalid numeric argument prints \f[CR]0\f[R] and sets status
\f[CR]1\f[R], a missing format returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and a width or
precision over \f[CR]16777216\f[R] is an error.
The numeric locale comes from the first nonempty value of
\f[CR]LC_ALL\f[R], \f[CR]LC_NUMERIC\f[R], and \f[CR]LANG\f[R] at each
call.
T}
T{
\f[CR]eval [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Joins arguments with spaces and runs the result in the current shell
(status of the last command, \f[CR]0\f[R] for empty input, \f[CR]2\f[R]
with an \f[CR]eval:\f[R] location on a syntax error).
T}
T{
\f[CR]exec [\-cl] [\-a name] [command [arg ...]]\f[R]
T}@T{
Replaces the shell with \f[CR]command\f[R] (\f[CR]\-a\f[R] sets argument
zero, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] prefixes it with a dash, \f[CR]\-c\f[R] clears the
environment), or applies its redirections to the shell when no command
is given.
A command that is not found or cannot run ends a non\-interactive shell
with \f[CR]127\f[R] or \f[CR]126\f[R] in every mood.
A restricted shell refuses \f[CR]exec\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]exit [n]\f[R], \f[CR]logout [n]\f[R], \f[CR]return [n]\f[R]  
T}@T{
Runs the \f[CR]EXIT\f[R] trap and exits with \f[CR]n\f[R] modulo
\f[CR]256\f[R] or the last status (\f[CR]exit\f[R]; a non\-numeric
operand returns \f[CR]2\f[R] without exiting, except in \f[CR]sh\f[R]
where the shell exits with \f[CR]2\f[R], and more than one operand exits
with \f[CR]1\f[R]).
\f[CR]logout\f[R] behaves as \f[CR]exit\f[R] in a login shell and
reports an error and returns \f[CR]1\f[R] elsewhere.
\f[CR]return\f[R] leaves the running function or sourced file with
\f[CR]n\f[R] modulo \f[CR]256\f[R]; outside them it returns
\f[CR]2\f[R], except in \f[CR]sh\f[R] where it ends a non\-interactive
shell with \f[CR]n\f[R].
A non\-numeric operand returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and ends a non\-interactive
shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]\f[R],
\f[CR]fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]\f[R]
T}@T{
Lists (\f[CR]\-l\f[R], previous sixteen events by default,
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] without numbers, \f[CR]\-r\f[R] reversed), edits, and
reruns history events selected by number, negative offset, or prefix.
The editor comes from \f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]FCEDIT\f[R], or
\f[CR]EDITOR\f[R], defaulting to \f[CR]vi\f[R] (\f[CR]ed\f[R] in the
POSIX moods); \f[CR]\-\f[R] runs the event unedited.
\f[CR]fc \-s\f[R] applies each literal replacement to one event, prints
it to standard error, runs it, and returns its status.
A text that matches no event returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]history [\-cnrwaps] [\-d selector] [operand]\f[R],
\f[CR]history \-S\f[R]
T}@T{
Lists events (newest \f[CR]N\f[R] with a numeric operand), loads the
history file (\f[CR]\-r\f[R], only new events with \f[CR]\-n\f[R]),
writes it (\f[CR]\-w\f[R]), appends missing private events
(\f[CR]\-a\f[R]), replaces the private list with the shared file
(\f[CR]\-S\f[R]), clears the private list (\f[CR]\-c\f[R]), prints
(\f[CR]\-p\f[R]) or stores (\f[CR]\-s\f[R]) its operands, and deletes
events by number, negative offset, or inclusive range (\f[CR]\-d\f[R]).
A restricted shell refuses file operands.
T}
T{
\f[CR]getopts optstring name [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Parses the next option from the arguments or positional parameters into
\f[CR]name\f[R], its argument into \f[CR]OPTARG\f[R], and the next index
into \f[CR]OPTIND\f[R].
A letter followed by \f[CR]:\f[R] takes an argument.
An unknown option sets \f[CR]name\f[R] to \f[CR]?\f[R] and prints a
diagnostic, which \f[CR]OPTERR=0\f[R] or a leading \f[CR]:\f[R] in
\f[CR]optstring\f[R] silences (the leading \f[CR]:\f[R] also stores the
letter in \f[CR]OPTARG\f[R] and sets \f[CR]name\f[R] to \f[CR]:\f[R] for
a missing argument).
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] while options remain and \f[CR]1\f[R] at their end.
T}
T{
\f[CR]kill [\-s sig | \-n num | \-sig] pid|job ...\f[R],
\f[CR]kill \-l [sig|status ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends a signal (\f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] by default, given by number or by
name with or without \f[CR]SIG\f[R]) to each process or job.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists signals or translates numbers, statuses above
\f[CR]128\f[R], and names.
No operand returns \f[CR]2\f[R], and an invalid signal or a failed
delivery returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] for an invalid signal in the
\f[CR]sh\f[R] mood).
T}
T{
\f[CR]let expr ...\f[R]
T}@T{
Evaluates each expression as described under Arithmetic evaluation,
returning \f[CR]0\f[R] when the last value is nonzero and \f[CR]1\f[R]
when it is zero or absent.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood has no \f[CR]let\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]mapfile [options] [array]\f[R],
\f[CR]readarray [options] [array]\f[R]
T}@T{
Stores lines of standard input in an indexed array (\f[CR]MAPFILE\f[R]
by default).
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] strips delimiters, \f[CR]\-n\f[R] limits the count
(\f[CR]0\f[R] for every line), \f[CR]\-s\f[R] skips leading lines,
\f[CR]\-O\f[R] sets the first index, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets the delimiter
byte (NUL when empty), \f[CR]\-u\f[R] reads a descriptor, and
\f[CR]\-C\f[R] runs a callback with the index and line every
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] lines (\f[CR]5000\f[R] by default).
T}
T{
\f[CR]newgrp [group]\f[R]
T}@T{
Replaces the shell with the \f[CR]newgrp\f[R] program from
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] (\f[CR]127\f[R] when missing, \f[CR]126\f[R] when it
cannot run).
A restricted shell refuses \f[CR]newgrp\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]read [\-ers] [\-a array] [\-d delim] [\-n|\-N count] [\-p prompt] [\-t timeout] [\-u fd] [\-q] [name ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Reads a line and splits it at \f[CR]IFS\f[R], giving one field to each
\f[CR]name\f[R] and the rest of the line to the last (\f[CR]REPLY\f[R]
without a name).
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] keeps backslashes, \f[CR]\-a\f[R] fills an indexed array,
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets the delimiter (NUL when empty), \f[CR]\-n\f[R] sets
a maximum byte count, \f[CR]\-N\f[R] reads exactly that many bytes
unsplit, \f[CR]\-t\f[R] sets a fractional timeout, \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
prompts at a terminal, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] disables echo, \f[CR]\-u\f[R]
reads a descriptor, \f[CR]\-q\f[R] reads one byte and returns
\f[CR]0\f[R] for \f[CR]y\f[R] or \f[CR]Y\f[R], and \f[CR]\-e\f[R] is
accepted without effect.
Returns \f[CR]1\f[R] at end of input (even after storing a partial line)
and \f[CR]142\f[R] after a timeout.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood requires a name and accepts only \f[CR]\-r\f[R]
and \f[CR]\-p\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]set [\-+abefhkmnprtuvxBCEHPTIARNSGW] [\-+o option] [\-M mood] [\-L moods] [\-\-] [arg ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Changes options (a minus enables, a plus disables) and replaces the
positional parameters with the arguments after the options or after
\f[CR]\-\-\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\f[R] also ends the options).
Without arguments prints the shell variables as sorted assignments;
\f[CR]set \-o\f[R] prints option states and \f[CR]set +o\f[R] prints
reusable commands.
\f[CR]set \-o option\f[R] accepts only Bash spellings, and a long option
such as \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] is rejected with a pointer to
\f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] turns restricted mode on and \f[CR]+r\f[R] fails with
\f[CR]1\f[R].
An unknown option returns \f[CR]1\f[R] in the Bash moods and ends a
non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]1\f[R] in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and
\f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R].
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R], a value other than the one held for
\f[CR]nounset\f[R], \f[CR]pipefail\f[R], \f[CR]failglob\f[R], or
\f[CR]nullglob\f[R] is an error that ends the script with \f[CR]1\f[R].
Letters and Koshka forms are listed below the table.
T}
T{
\f[CR]shift [n]\f[R]
T}@T{
Discards the first \f[CR]n\f[R] positional parameters (default
\f[CR]1\f[R]).
A count above \f[CR]$#\f[R] or below \f[CR]0\f[R] returns \f[CR]1\f[R],
and ends a non\-interactive shell with \f[CR]2\f[R] in the \f[CR]sh\f[R]
mood.
T}
T{
\f[CR]shopt [\-pqsuo] [option ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Shows or changes the options listed under \f[CR]shopt\f[R] options.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] enables and \f[CR]\-u\f[R] disables, or lists the options
that are on or off without operands.
Without \f[CR]\-s\f[R] or \f[CR]\-u\f[R], prints each state and returns
\f[CR]1\f[R] when one is off.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints reusable commands, \f[CR]\-o\f[R] selects the
\f[CR]set \-o\f[R] options, and \f[CR]\-q\f[R] prints nothing.
An unknown option returns \f[CR]1\f[R] after the valid ones apply.
T}
T{
\f[CR]time [\-p] [\-R] pipeline\f[R], \f[CR]times\f[R] 
T}@T{
Times a pipeline (\f[CR]time\f[R], a reserved word, see Pipelines;
hidden in \f[CR]sh\f[R]) or prints the user and system times of the
shell, then of its children (\f[CR]times\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]trap [\-lp] [[action] condition ...]\f[R]
T}@T{
Sets \f[CR]action\f[R] for each signal (by number or name, with or
without \f[CR]SIG\f[R]) or condition: \f[CR]EXIT\f[R] or \f[CR]0\f[R] at
exit, \f[CR]ERR\f[R] after a failing command where \f[CR]errexit\f[R]
would act, \f[CR]DEBUG\f[R] before each simple command, and
\f[CR]RETURN\f[R] at the end of a function or sourced file.
An empty action ignores the signal and \f[CR]\-\f[R] restores the
default.
Without arguments or with \f[CR]\-p\f[R], prints reusable
\f[CR]trap\f[R] commands, and \f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists signals.
A trap action runs with the status of the preceding command and leaves
that status unchanged.
An invalid signal returns \f[CR]1\f[R].
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects \f[CR]ERR\f[R], \f[CR]RETURN\f[R],
\f[CR]\-l\f[R], and \f[CR]\-p\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]ulimit [\-HSa] [\-cdflmnpstuvw] [limit]\f[R]
T}@T{
Shows or sets a resource limit (file size \f[CR]\-f\f[R] by default),
with \f[CR]\-H\f[R] or \f[CR]\-S\f[R] selecting the hard or soft limit
and \f[CR]\-a\f[R] printing all.
The resources are core size (\f[CR]\-c\f[R]), data size
(\f[CR]\-d\f[R]), file size (\f[CR]\-f\f[R]), locked memory
(\f[CR]\-l\f[R]), resident set (\f[CR]\-m\f[R]), open files
(\f[CR]\-n\f[R]), pipe size (\f[CR]\-p\f[R]), stack size
(\f[CR]\-s\f[R]), CPU seconds (\f[CR]\-t\f[R]), processes
(\f[CR]\-u\f[R]), virtual memory (\f[CR]\-v\f[R]), and file locks
(\f[CR]\-w\f[R]).
A limit is a number or \f[CR]unlimited\f[R], and an invalid limit or one
above the hard limit returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in
\f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
T{
\f[CR]umask [\-pS] [mask]\f[R]
T}@T{
Shows or sets the file creation mask in octal or symbolic form
(\f[CR]u=rwx,g=rx,o=\f[R]).
\f[CR]\-S\f[R] prints it symbolically and \f[CR]\-p\f[R] as a reusable
command.
An invalid mask returns \f[CR]1\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R] in \f[CR]sh\f[R]).
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]set\f[B] option letters\f[R] \- \f[CR]\-a\f[R] allexport,
\f[CR]\-b\f[R] notify (reports a finished job at once while the editor
waits), \f[CR]\-e\f[R] errexit, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] noglob, \f[CR]\-h\f[R]
hashall (accepted without effect), \f[CR]\-k\f[R] keyword (places
assignment arguments in the command environment), \f[CR]\-m\f[R] monitor
(job control), \f[CR]\-n\f[R] noexec, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] privileged,
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] onecmd (stops after one top\-level command),
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] nounset, \f[CR]\-v\f[R] verbose, \f[CR]\-x\f[R] xtrace,
\f[CR]\-B\f[R] braceexpand, \f[CR]\-C\f[R] noclobber, \f[CR]\-E\f[R]
errtrace, \f[CR]\-H\f[R] histexpand, \f[CR]\-P\f[R] physical,
\f[CR]\-T\f[R] functrace.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Options without a letter are \f[CR]emacs\f[R], \f[CR]vi\f[R],
\f[CR]history\f[R], \f[CR]ignoreeof\f[R],
\f[CR]interactive\-comments\f[R], \f[CR]nolog\f[R] (accepted without
effect), \f[CR]pipefail\f[R], and \f[CR]posix\f[R].
\f[CR]set \-o posix\f[R] selects the \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] mood, and
\f[CR]set +o posix\f[R] selects \f[CR]bash\f[R] from
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] or \f[CR]sh\f[R].
The options are described under \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] options.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]set\f[B] Koshka forms\f[R] \- \f[CR]set \-M MOOD\f[R] and
\f[CR]set \-L MOOD,...\f[R] select the mood and source the startup files
of other moods as the command\-line options do; without a value they
print the mood or the initialized moods.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]\-I\f[R], \f[CR]\-A\f[R], \f[CR]\-R\f[R], \f[CR]\-N\f[R],
\f[CR]\-S\f[R], and \f[CR]\-G\f[R] set
\f[CR]interpreter.mimic_shebang\f[R],
\f[CR]debug.print_syntax_tree\f[R],
\f[CR]debug.print_lexed_word_escapes\f[R],
\f[CR]debug.report_every_exit_code\f[R],
\f[CR]debug.print_evaluation_statistics\f[R], and
\f[CR]debug.print_memory_report_at_exit\f[R].
\f[CR]\-W\f[R], \f[CR]\-WW\f[R], and \f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] set
\f[CR]optimizer.warning_level\f[R].
Every other Koshka setting belongs to \f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Name references (\f[CB]declare \-n\f[B])\f[R] \- A reference holds
the target variable in its value, which may carry a subscript.
Expansion, assignment, append, export, and unset act on the target,
following chains, with the target\[aq]s read\-only, restricted, integer,
and case rules.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]${!name}\f[R] gives the target, \f[CR]unset \-n\f[R] and
\f[CR]+n\f[R] remove the reference, and \f[CR][[ \-R name ]]\f[R] is
true for a reference with a value.
A reference without a value binds at its first assignment.
.IP \[bu] 2
A self\-reference is an error at top level.
A circular chain expands as unset without a message, and an assignment
through it is an error.
A read\-only variable, \f[CR]PATH\f[R], \f[CR]IFS\f[R], and a dynamic
variable other than \f[CR]RANDOM\f[R] and \f[CR]SECONDS\f[R] cannot
become a reference.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]complete\f[B] specs\f[R] \- A spec offers the candidates of
its actions, \f[CR]\-G\f[R] glob, \f[CR]\-W\f[R] words, and, on an
explicit Tab, the \f[CR]COMPREPLY\f[R] of its \f[CR]\-F\f[R] function
and the output lines of its \f[CR]\-C\f[R] command, in that order.
The \f[CR]\-C\f[R] command runs as a command substitution with the
command, the word, and the previous word as arguments and
\f[CR]COMP_LINE\f[R], \f[CR]COMP_POINT\f[R], \f[CR]COMP_KEY\f[R], and
\f[CR]COMP_TYPE\f[R] exported.
A completion function receives the same arguments and the
\f[CR]COMP_\f[R] variables described under Shell variables.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Trailing spaces are removed from each \f[CR]COMPREPLY\f[R] entry, the
spec\[aq]s \f[CR]\-X\f[R], \f[CR]\-P\f[R], and \f[CR]\-S\f[R] apply, and
with the \f[CR]filenames\f[R] option a slash is appended to each
directory.
The ghost suggestion never runs a function or command.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]complete \-o\f[B] options\f[R] \- \f[CR]default\f[R] and
\f[CR]bashdefault\f[R] fall back to filenames and to the shell
completion when the spec finds nothing, \f[CR]filenames\f[R] treats
candidates as filenames, \f[CR]dirnames\f[R] adds matching directories
when nothing else matched, and \f[CR]plusdirs\f[R] adds them always.
\f[CR]fullquote\f[R] quotes every candidate, \f[CR]noquote\f[R] none,
\f[CR]nosort\f[R] keeps the generated order, and \f[CR]nospace\f[R] adds
no space.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
A spec quotes inserted candidates only when it generates filenames, as
in Bash.
An empty result falls through to the next completion source.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]complete \-D\f[B], \f[CB]\-E\f[B], \f[CB]\-I\f[B]\f[R] \-
With \f[CR]progcomp_alias\f[R] on, a command without a spec uses the
spec of the first word of its alias, and an explicit Tab follows a
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] command to its symlink target.
A command still without a spec uses the \f[CR]\-D\f[R] spec, and when
its function returns \f[CR]124\f[R] completion restarts with the spec it
registered.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]\-E\f[R] serves an explicit Tab on an empty line and
\f[CR]\-I\f[R] the command word.
Their functions receive \f[CR]_EmptycmD_\f[R] and
\f[CR]_InitialWorD_\f[R] as the command.
Of several of \f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-E\f[R], and \f[CR]\-I\f[R], the
first in that order applies.
.RE
.SS Locale
The first nonempty value of \f[CR]LC_ALL\f[R], \f[CR]LC_CTYPE\f[R], and
\f[CR]LANG\f[R] selects the character locale.
A \f[CR]UTF\-8\f[R] or \f[CR]utf8\f[R] codeset selects UTF\-8 when the
platform supports it, and anything else selects bytes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Character semantics\f[R] \- In UTF\-8, \f[CR]?\f[R] and each step
of \f[CR]*\f[R] consume one character, bracket expressions compare code
points, \f[CR]${#v}\f[R] and \f[CR]${v:offset:length}\f[R] count
characters, and \f[CR]${v\[ha]}\f[R], \f[CR]${v,}\f[R], and
\f[CR]${v\[ti]}\f[R] change the case of multibyte letters.
This applies to pathname expansion, \f[CR]case\f[R],
\f[CR][[ == ]]\f[R], \f[CR]${v#pat}\f[R], \f[CR]${v%pat}\f[R],
\f[CR]${v/pat/rep}\f[R], \f[CR]${v\[ha]pat}\f[R], and
\f[CR]compgen \-X\f[R].
Completion always matches by UTF\-8 sequence.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Collation and numbers\f[R] \- In the Bash moods, pathname expansion
sorts by the collation order of \f[CR]LC_ALL\f[R] or
\f[CR]LC_COLLATE\f[R], read from the environment the first time a
pattern is sorted.
The first nonempty value of \f[CR]LC_ALL\f[R], \f[CR]LC_NUMERIC\f[R],
and \f[CR]LANG\f[R] selects the numeric locale of \f[CR]printf\f[R].
.SS Restricted shell
A shell started with \f[CR]\-r\f[R] or as \f[CR]rbash\f[R] enters
restricted mode after its startup files finish.
No setting turns restricted mode off, child shells inherit it, and
sourced scripts keep it.
A shell script found through \f[CR]PATH\f[R] and run as a command runs
without it.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Restricted area
T}@T{
Rejected operations
T}
_
T{
Directories and variables
T}@T{
Directory changes (including \f[CR]pushd\f[R], \f[CR]popd\f[R], and
\f[CR]z\f[R]), and changes to \f[CR]SHELL\f[R], \f[CR]PATH\f[R],
\f[CR]ENV\f[R], and \f[CR]BASH_ENV\f[R], which become read\-only.
T}
T{
Commands and paths
T}@T{
A directory separator in a command word or \f[CR]source\f[R] operand,
\f[CR]exec\f[R], \f[CR]newgrp\f[R], \f[CR]bench\f[R],
\f[CR]assimilate\f[R], \f[CR]slurp\f[R], \f[CR]hash \-p\f[R],
\f[CR]command \-p\f[R], \f[CR]enable \-f\f[R], \f[CR]enable \-d\f[R],
and every form of \f[CR]koshkit\f[R].
T}
T{
Redirections
T}@T{
\f[CR]>\f[R], \f[CR]>|\f[R], \f[CR]<>\f[R], \f[CR]>>\f[R],
\f[CR]&>\f[R], \f[CR]&>>\f[R], and \f[CR]>&\f[R] to a file.
Numeric descriptor duplication stays available.
T}
T{
Settings
T}@T{
\f[CR]set \-M\f[R], \f[CR]set \-I\f[R], \f[CR]set +I\f[R],
\f[CR]set +r\f[R], \f[CR]source \-\-mood\f[R], history file operands,
and the \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] forms \f[CR]create\f[R], \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]load\f[R], and \f[CR]save \-\-persist\f[R].
\f[CR]set \-o posix\f[R] stays available.
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Bundled utilities\f[R] \- A command word falls back only to a
bundled utility that a startup file enabled with \f[CR]enable\f[R].
After startup, \f[CR]enable\f[R] can disable a builtin but cannot enable
one or change a bundled utility.
An enabled utility that starts other programs, such as \f[CR]env\f[R],
\f[CR]xargs\f[R], \f[CR]find\f[R], \f[CR]nohup\f[R], \f[CR]timeout\f[R],
or \f[CR]watch\f[R], can start any program, so a startup file for a
restricted shell should enable only utilities that start none.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Settings files\f[R] \- A restricted shell ignores and removes
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] with a warning and applies only presentation and
diagnostic options from the settings files.
It ignores the mood, semantic options,
\f[CR]editor.history.file_path\f[R], and
\f[CR]editor.history.max_entries\f[R] there with a warning.
.SS Related shells
The \f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]dash\f[R], \f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]fish\f[R],
and \f[CR]busybox\f[R] manuals describe their respective shells.
